Provably fair systems let you check that a game's outcome wasn't altered after your bet was placed, using a cryptographic seed exchange. It's not the same as third-party RNG certification, which some sites also carry for their slot and table titles — check which one a site actually offers rather than assuming both.
Several sites on this page, including LuckyRollers with its included sports bets and Vave with its free bet offer, run a sportsbook alongside the casino. These typically use the same crypto wallet and account, so deposits, withdrawals and no-KYC thresholds usually carry across both products — but bonus terms for casino play and sports betting are almost always separate, with different wagering rules attached to each.
None of the sites reviewed on this page are licensed or regulated by any US federal or state authority. They operate under offshore licences, most commonly from Curaçao or Anjouan, and serve US players in a legal grey area rather than as a regulated product. A handful of US states run their own licensed online casinos — New Jersey, Pennsylvania, Michigan, West Virginia, Connecticut, Delaware and Rhode Island among them — but none of those regulated operators run on crypto, and that regulated framework doesn't extend to the offshore sites covered here.
Availability differs by state, and offshore operators commonly self-restrict certain states in their own terms of service. It's on the player to check their own state's gambling laws before signing up anywhere on this page — we don't state that any site is legal in a specific state, and you should treat that as unresolved until you've checked it yourself. Every site listed here sets its own minimum age, either 18+ or 21+ depending on the operator and any applicable state rule, and that minimum is non-negotiable regardless of how casually a site presents itself.
""No KYC"" describes a threshold, not a permanent state. Typical patterns across the sites we reviewed:
Minimal verification is a genuine privacy and convenience feature for players who don't want to hand over documents to play casually. It is not a mechanism for hiding income, avoiding taxes, or getting around sanctions or anti-money-laundering checks, and no site on this page should be used with that intent. US players remain liable for tax on gambling winnings and on crypto gains regardless of how a site verifies them — speak with a qualified tax professional about your own obligations rather than assuming anonymity changes them.
Every welcome offer listed on this page — from CoinPoker's match bonus to Wild.io's multi-deposit package — comes with wagering (playthrough) requirements that must be cleared before bonus funds or winnings from them can be withdrawn. Cashback and rakeback offers, like those from Winna and Thrill, usually calculate against net losses over a set period rather than total wagered volume, which is a different mechanic worth understanding before comparing headline percentages across sites. Time limits and maximum bet restrictions while wagering are common too — check the specific terms on the operator's site before depositing.
Crypto casinos carry two layered risks: the normal risk of losing your stake, and the added risk that crypto's price can move against you between depositing and cashing out. Neither risk is eliminated by picking a well-rated site, and no game or strategy guarantees a return. If gambling stops feeling like entertainment, the Responsible Gambling page has tools for setting limits, and the National Problem Gambling Helpline (1-800-GAMBLER) is available for confidential support. Offshore sites may not offer the same self-exclusion tools or player protections that state-regulated US casinos are required to provide, so it's worth setting your own limits before you start rather than relying on the site to do it for you.
If anonymity and payout speed matter most, weigh the no-KYC thresholds and withdrawal timing above everything else. If you're chasing bonus value, read the wagering requirements alongside the headline percentage rather than the percentage alone. And if you're new to crypto itself, start by getting comfortable with wallet setup and network fees before you deposit anywhere. Our About Us page explains how we approach these reviews, and our Contact Us page is there if you have questions we haven't covered.",ok
politifact15349,https://www.hollywoodreporter.com/live-feed/daily-shows-michelle-wolf-lands-her-own-netflix-late-night-show-1083705,'The Daily Show's' Michelle Wolf Lands Her Own Netflix Late-Night Show (Exclusive),963097193405210624	963097410124775427	963097532766269440	963097790497738759	963097841240428544	963097890561273856	963097928322580480	963097928175898625	963097935134171141	963098657959698433	963099635320590336	963100283894026240	963100688233398272	963102188988805121	963103754005475330	963103820204081152	963106738604974080	963107004142284805	963111747480641536	963127629330120704	963135486364565504	963184670925115392	963196981148450816	963267207156334592	963292885528600578	963418009208524800	963420370089979905	963453222139650052	963467255601344512	963663130017587202	967377001970417665	967377732639354892	969210919631904768	974784649837756416	977305034286231552	991157669959479297,1,hollywoodreporter.com,82,"Michelle Wolf has landed a show of her own.
After nearly half a decade working on Late Night With Seth Meyers and The Daily Show With Trevor Noah , Wolf is set to host her own weekly show on Netflix . The streaming giant, which is making a considerable push in the talk-show space with entries from David Letterman and Joel McHale , is billing Wolf’s show as a break from the seriousness of late-night comedy. The planned star vehicle will launch later this year.
The 32-year-old rising star, whose show does not yet have a name, is expected to make fun of everything and everybody, with no preaching or political agenda — unless, of course, it’s funny. “You can expect the types of jokes my former bosses would tell me we couldn’t do on TV,” Wolf joked in a statement, with Netflix’s vp content Bela Bajaria , adding more earnestly: “We’re thrilled to be working with Michelle, a gifted writer and performer with a singular voice.”
The announcement comes some two months after Wolf earned raves for her first stand-up special, Nice Lady, which aired on rival HBO. The special gave Wolf her highest-profile platform to date to tackle subjects ranging from feminism (“I want equal pay! And a chardonnay”) to dating and other social issues.
Though Wolf is still a relative fresh face on the stand-up circuit, she has already earned the respect of several comedic heavyweights, or former heavyweights, including Louis C.K., who tapped her to open for him on his 2016 stand-up tour and to act in his celebrated web show Horace and Pete , and Chris Rock, who hired her to write for his Oscars ceremony.
By jumping to Netflix, Wolf marks the latest star to exit Comedy Central — and in her case, as it was for predecessors like John Oliver, just as her career is taking off. In addition to her contributor duties on the cable network’s Daily Show, Wolf created and starred in two digital series, Now Hiring and Used People, for the Viacom network.
Looking ahead, Wolf’s Netflix show, a rare and much-welcomed female-fronted series in what is still a male-dominated space, will be produced via her Cats in Pants banner. Dan Powell ( Inside Amy Schumer ) and Christine Nangle ( The President’s Show, The Mick ) will serve as co-showrunners , with her manager Daniel Bodansky joining them as an executive producer.
Wolf is repped by UTA , Dixon Talent and Hansen Jacobson; Powell is with UTA , Odenkirk Provissiero and Hansen Jacobson; and Nangle is repped by UTA and Jackoway Tyerman .
