gossipcop-856558,https://www.vanityfair.com/style/2017/08/inside-the-private-celebrity-friendly-terminal-at-lax,"Inside the Private, Celebrity-Friendly Terminal at LAX",867366454009745408	867367056747790336	867367184682467328	867368112290770946	867368156335030272	867368163976945664	867368163209388038	867368161724649476	867368166027952128	867368176580927493	867369006918807553	867369143858855936	867369615135051780	867370002034315264	867370903138476033	867370910474424320	867370926404325376	867370925490028544	867370933438210048	867371204843372545	867371520926007296	867371547438178305	867372444515835904	867378358925000705	867380470195126272	867385168255893504	867385532447424514	867385795430055936	867386288265207808	867386789954301952	867389508085399552	867389513420619776	867389512866865152	867389515853291520	867389514561372162	867389519988797440	867389519938506752	867389518332051456	867394290405838848	867400148569214976	867400621216993280	867400829929705472	867521114607570944	867526892965867521	867763427753697280	867908168256552960	868315869331566596	884903436562694144	957746656119844864,0,vanityfair.com,54,"Once upon a time, flying was special. Glamorous even. Three-quarter-length minks, leather and pigskin palms, fedoras and double-breasted tweed. Suited and booted was how it was done on the daily. Seats on a plane were spacious, the food was real, the lines manageable. Eye contact was mandatory, and a handshake was as stiff as the drinks that were served.
And then—oil, terror, dollars and cents—times changed. Nothing personifies the anxiety-inducing madness of modern air travel like an international terminal. This is why we need Xanax and Ambien over pretzels and peanuts.
The American airport is a case study of the herd mentality, where waiting and woe have replaced whimsical and wishful. Nothing works, no one cares, and nothing seems to improve security or efficiency despite pre-checks and precautions. Footwear to laptops and lithium—we’re all resigned to our fate when flying, just hoping not to get too fucked over from point A to B. Nostalgia be damned, some things were better before the age of apps, and traveling was one of them. Flying was special.
And now, for the most elite of the jet set, it is again. In May, the first private terminal in the U.S. opened at Los Angeles International Airport . Private is the operative word; the $22 million facility is located on the far side of the airport, away from public terminals and paparazzi. There are 13 suites with bathrooms, drinks, food, televisions, and a staff of eight servants—er, attendants.
Passengers complete security screening and clear customs in the terminal before being driven, head-of-state style, across the runway to the plane in a BMW 7 Series sedan (supposedly just 70 steps from terminal to plane. The average Joe hoofs more than 2,000).
Modeled on Heathrow’s Windsor Suite, which runs about $3,500 for membership, V.I.P. terminals are catching on, with one in Dubai that opened last December, and options in Amsterdam, Munich, and now Los Angeles, where the chosen few no longer deal with luggage, lines, or paparazzi. Could anything be more celebrity-friendly in a city known for stars?
V.I.P. is a descriptor so casually thrown around these days that any place with burgundy velvet and a bald bouncer can call itself “exclusive.” But the LAX private terminal is crammed with tangible reminders of how different the other half lives. Organic seaweed snacks, 85 percent cacao, Laurent-Perrier Champagne, scented candles, daybeds. Passengers can take advantage of seemingly every toiletterie and travel hack under the sun, from universal chargers and noise-canceling headphones to Emergen-C and roll-on deodorant. Instead of rushing to the gate and jockeying for a seat, the biggest problem in this terminal is figuring out whether there is any Dijon mustard (there is), and how to switch from CNN to MSNBC on the suite’s TV.
During a visit to the terminal in June I met the suite’s designer, Gavin de Becker —sort of. I was led to an empty room, and a James Bond-esque live tablet. De Becker, 63, with jet-black hair, sharp eyes even across a screen, and wearing what looked like a very comfortable kimono, was calling across the Pacific from Tokyo.
“What others have missed,” he began, “is that this is for business travelers. This isn’t luxury. This is logistics. You can park your car and leave it, book spa services, doctors, order food in advance, everything you could get in the terminal, without having to carry bags, walking to a gate change or avoiding lines.”
De Becker has made a career in the world of security, shepherding high-value clients, people worried about watching their backs. He’s written a best-seller, The Gift of Fear, gushed on Oprah’s couch multiple times, and spent decades protecting A-listers (one story goes that a character from the original Twin Peaks was based on him). He’s very savvy and well-connected—he gave eulogies for both Carrie Fisher and Garry Shandling—having earned the trust of the kind of L.A. denizens who might pay a premium for this kind of concierge service.
If the mood in LAX is base panic, in de Becker’s suite it’s sublime peace. To that end, there’s a small art gallery filled with photos from Gary Yost, a series of olive-green Anna’s hummingbirds feeding their young. There’s a park onsite with tents and a faux fireplace, and a kid’s area to play on real grass—nowhere else in the world does such a thing exist with planes flying overhead, all on the runway. Each suite has a toy menu. Lucky kids. And I didn’t see any during my stay. That’s the beauty of it—I didn’t see anyone. You’re not supposed to.
Membership is $7,500 a year, and members pay an additional $2,700 for domestic flights, $3,000 for international flights, per use. (Prices are slated to change in 2018.) That allows for four guests at no additional cost, plus free parking, car detailing, pre-flight massages, in-suite manicures, haircuts, and preferential pricing XOJET charters. Non-members can participate at $3,500 for domestic, $4,000 per international flights (with service for up to three others).
It’s a lot more expensive than in-flight Wi-Fi or a first-class upgrade, but de Becker insists it’s cost effective for, say, a weekly traveler to New York. “Business travelers no longer have to charter services, so it’s actually a cost-saver,” he says. “You can spend your time walking LAX or you can spend your time working.”
If the mood in LAX is base panic, in de Becker’s suite it’s sublime peace.
And for the business travelers often departing Los Angeles, the privacy—and security—is paramount. “If you’re Johnny Depp at LAX,” de Becker says, “the whole T.S.A. line falls apart.” But if you make $20 million per pirate picture, a couple grand is chump change. And for deep-pocketed studios, talent agencies, and record labels—the places serving acts eager to flee the everyday Instagram feed—this seems like the best perk possible.
De Becker says he didn’t do a single bit of P.R. for the launch. He doesn’t want the masses—he wants high-value. He wants exclusivity.
But he’s also willing to let at least one non-V.I.P behind the curtain. “I want to try it,” I told him, mustering my courage. “One day, I want to be one of your clients.”
“I like that you asked, Adam,” de Becker, said laughing. “Most people don’t ask. I like that. And that’s why I’m going to let you in.”
I had an upcoming flight to New York, I said, before pushing further. I’m going to Bali in two weeks. Domestic flights are for the birds, I want the full treatment. Something in that ask tickled de Becker. His face softened, he showed some teeth. He nodded his head, deciding. And with one word he changed my travel future: “Done.”
My flight was at 3:45 P.M., and my scheduled departure from the suite was at 2:45. By private terminal standards, that was interminable travel time. “Flights to the Middle East sometimes have extra security protocols,” said my escort, Luke, in all-black with a secret service earpiece and a square jaw.
After the world’s shortest and smoothest T.S.A. screening—with one smiling agent, and they never smile—I stepped into the back of a shiny black beemer and rolled onto the runway.
It’s hard to describe the strange awe of riding 20 m.p.h. (that’s the speed limit) while jets rumble outside your tinted window. There’s a surprising number of vehicles on the runway: cleaning crews, food services, maintenance, you name it. It was a back-of-the-house revelation. There are even what could very well be hiking trails tucked away amid LAX’s 3,650 acres.
But the biggest surprise was that we had to make a stop before the plane, and that was at Qatar’s gate. Passengers had to check in here before being bused to the plane. Bused.
We parked, car still running cop style, and Luke led with key card in hand, opening doors labeled “Security,” whisking me to the gate agent to verify my ticket. I felt eyes drilling into my scalp, a sea of people wondering, “Who is that?”
“Pay them no mind,” my inner Louis XVI commanded. “Let them eat it and just act like you belong.”
This is probably the closest one can get to feeling like a celeb without the agent and entourage.
The gate agent didn’t appear to have any qualms when Luke explained I wouldn’t be riding the bus. “We like to take our clients direct to the gate,” he repeated. The agent shrugged, checked my ticket, then gave the O.K. and Luke and I rushed back to the car before the petit bourgeois had a chance to revolt.
We drove along the runway, right up to a Boeing 777. QR 740. I watched the cleaning crew wrap, maintenance pump gas into the wings, and under them, the engine’s hypnotic rotating pinwheel.
Luke put his hand to his earpiece, then told me it was time. I got up and exited the car. “This way.” I walked up a staircase, the first one to board the plane. Luke wouldn’t even let me carry my bag. At the top of the stairs, I took the bag and shook Luke’s hand. “My pleasure.”
This is probably the closest one can get to feeling like a celeb without the agent and entourage. At takeoff, I wasn’t fantasizing about Balinese beaches. I was visualizing how quickly I’d breeze through customs on my return trip, the time I’d save. This was the trip. I’d never had anything like it. And I wanted more.",ok
