gossipcop-2615005056,www.celebritynetworth.com/richest-businessmen/lawyers/amal-clooney-net-worth/,Amal Clooney Net Worth,732184852385038342	732184860287082497	732184867979452417	762270617890463744	762270623380799488	813870617183354880	813888111063289860	821793567546871808	872149420095270912	872153009484058624	872153023388065792	877927505713283072	900134479196540928	966408601693716482	989975765944078336	995646091206053889	995703307271311361	996353333257531392	998883422268416003	999355108784852992	1005083612142821376	1009491756604420096	1009744575483670531	1014579673899196416	1023929992781225985	1023940411054874629	1023961429974511621	1023991866386276352	1024022065186308096	1024052264045080581	1032565340545241089	1035529897282691072	1035560091720679424,1,celebritynetworth.com,22,"Amal Clooney is a Lebanese-British barrister, human rights lawyer, author, and academic who has a net worth of $50 million.
Amal Clooney specializes in public international law, international criminal law, and human rights and has represented governments, political prisoners, journalists, survivors of mass atrocities, and victims of sexual violence before courts and international institutions around the world. Her clients and cases have included Nobel Peace Prize winner Nadia Murad and other Yazidi survivors of ISIS, Nobel laureate Maria Ressa, Reuters journalists Wa Lone and Kyaw Soe Oo, former Maldives president Mohamed Nasheed, and victims of atrocities in Darfur. She has also served in advisory roles for the United Nations and the British government.
Outside the legal world, Amal is widely known for her marriage to actor George Clooney . The couple married in 2014, welcomed twins in 2017, and co-founded the Clooney Foundation for Justice, which provides free legal assistance in defense of free speech and women's rights in more than 40 countries. Amal has continued expanding her legal and academic work while becoming one of the world's most recognizable human rights attorneys. In 2025, she joined Oxford University's Blavatnik School of Government as a Professor of Practice in International Law and helped establish the Oxford Institute of Technology and Justice, which explores ways artificial intelligence and other technologies can improve access to justice.
Amal Alamuddin was born on February 3, 1978, in Beirut, Lebanon. Her family left Lebanon during the country's civil war when Amal was two years old and settled in Buckinghamshire, England. Her mother, Baria Alamuddin, became a journalist and editor, while her father, Ramzi Alamuddin, worked in business and later returned to Lebanon. Amal grew up with a younger sister and two half-brothers and attended Dr Challoner's High School.
She studied law at St Hugh's College at the University of Oxford, where she received academic honors and graduated in 2000. Amal then moved to the United States to earn a Master of Laws degree at New York University School of Law. During her time at NYU, she worked as a student clerk for Sonia Sotomayor , who was then serving as a judge on the U.S. Court of Appeals for the Second Circuit and later became a justice of the U.S. Supreme Court.
Clooney was admitted to the New York Bar in 2002 and began her career at the international law firm Sullivan & Cromwell in New York. She worked on litigation and investigations before moving into international public law and criminal law. She subsequently worked in The Hague at institutions including the International Court of Justice and the International Criminal Tribunal for the former Yugoslavia.
Amal later worked with the Office of the Prosecutor at the Special Tribunal for Lebanon, which was established to prosecute those responsible for the 2005 assassination of former Lebanese prime minister Rafic Hariri. She was called to the Bar of England and Wales in 2010 and joined Doughty Street Chambers in London. Her practice developed around international human rights, international criminal law, freedom of expression, and cases involving state responsibility.
Clooney has held several advisory positions connected to the United Nations and international institutions. She served as a senior adviser to former UN Secretary-General Kofi Annan when he was the UN-Arab League Joint Special Envoy for Syria and served as counsel to a UN inquiry examining the use of armed drones. She has also served on the United Kingdom's expert team focused on preventing sexual violence in conflict and was appointed to the UK Attorney General's panel of specialists in public international law.
In 2019, the British government appointed Clooney Special Envoy on Media Freedom. She helped establish a panel of international legal experts tasked with developing recommendations for governments seeking to strengthen protections for journalists. Amal resigned from the position in September 2020 after the British government introduced legislation that ministers acknowledged could breach international law in connection with the UK's withdrawal from the European Union.
A significant portion of Clooney's career has involved representing journalists and other individuals imprisoned or prosecuted because of their work. She represented Al Jazeera journalist Mohamed Fahmy after he was imprisoned in Egypt and represented Azerbaijani investigative journalist Khadija Ismayilova in proceedings before the European Court of Human Rights. She also worked on behalf of Reuters reporters Wa Lone and Kyaw Soe Oo, who were sentenced to seven years in prison in Myanmar after investigating the killing of Rohingya civilians and were released in 2019.
Clooney has served as international counsel for Filipino journalist and Nobel Peace Prize winner Maria Ressa, who has faced multiple prosecutions connected to her work with the news organization Rappler. Earlier in her career, she represented WikiLeaks founder Julian Assange in extradition proceedings in Britain. She has also represented former Maldives president Mohamed Nasheed and worked on cases involving individuals detained or prosecuted in Azerbaijan, Egypt, Myanmar, and other countries.
Clooney became particularly prominent for her work representing Yazidi victims of ISIS. Her clients have included Nadia Murad, who was kidnapped and enslaved by ISIS and later received the 2018 Nobel Peace Prize for her campaign against wartime sexual violence. Clooney advocated for the creation of a United Nations investigation into crimes committed by ISIS in Iraq and has worked on criminal and civil cases seeking accountability for genocide, enslavement, sexual violence, and other atrocities.
She represented the mother of a five-year-old Yazidi girl in a German prosecution that resulted in an ISIS member being convicted of genocide. The case was among the first criminal trials anywhere in the world to establish individual responsibility for the genocide of the Yazidi people. Clooney has represented victims in additional cases that resulted in convictions of ISIS members for genocide or crimes against humanity.
She also represents more than 800 Yazidi victims in litigation in the United States against French cement company Lafarge. The plaintiffs allege that payments and other support provided by Lafarge to ISIS and other armed groups contributed to terrorist acts committed against Yazidis. The litigation seeks damages under U.S. anti-terrorism laws.
Clooney has also represented victims of atrocities committed in Darfur. She served as counsel for more than 100 victims participating in the International Criminal Court prosecution of Ali Muhammad Ali Abd-Al-Rahman, also known as Ali Kushayb, a former commander associated with the Janjaweed militia. In October 2025, the ICC convicted Abd-Al-Rahman on 27 counts of war crimes and crimes against humanity, including murder, torture, persecution, and sexual violence.
Her international practice has included representing Armenia in proceedings concerning recognition and discussion of the Armenian genocide and participating in cases before the International Court of Justice. In 2022, Clooney led a legal task force advising the government of Ukraine on potential avenues for prosecuting war crimes and obtaining compensation for victims following Russia's invasion. Her work has increasingly centered on representing victims directly rather than limiting her role to government or institutional clients.
Clooney has combined legal practice with teaching and scholarship throughout her career. She taught at Columbia Law School as a visiting professor and co-taught courses dealing with human rights and international law. She has also lectured at institutions including The Hague Academy of International Law, the University of North Carolina, and SOAS University of London.
In 2025, Clooney was appointed a Professor of Practice in International Law at Oxford University's Blavatnik School of Government. She also became a senior fellow and co-founder of the Oxford Institute of Technology and Justice, which officially launched later that year as a partnership between Oxford and the Clooney Foundation for Justice. The institute focuses on using artificial intelligence and other technology to expand access to legal services, strengthen accountability for abuses, and protect fair-trial rights in increasingly digital justice systems.
Clooney has also written extensively on international law. She co-authored ""The Right to a Fair Trial in International Law"" with Philippa Webb, a book that received a Certificate of Merit from the American Society of International Law. In 2024, she co-edited ""Freedom of Speech in International Law"" with former UK Supreme Court president Lord David Neuberger.
Amal and George established the Clooney Foundation for Justice in 2016. The organization provides free legal assistance to people targeted because of their speech and to women and girls seeking protection from discriminatory laws, child marriage, sexual violence, and other abuses. Its work has expanded to more than 40 countries and includes thousands of hours of free legal representation.
One of the foundation's best-known programs is TrialWatch, launched in 2019 to monitor criminal trials involving journalists and other defendants at risk of unfair prosecution. The program trains trial monitors, documents potential violations of fair-trial standards, and provides legal assistance to defendants. The foundation says its work has contributed to the release or reduction of charges for dozens of journalists.
Another major program, Waging Justice for Women, provides legal support to women and girls challenging discrimination and gender-based violence. The initiative has supported litigation addressing child marriage, property rights, reproductive rights, workplace abuse, and violence against women. The foundation has also created fellowships for young women lawyers in Africa and helped establish legal-aid programs intended to expand women's access to representation.
Clooney has received numerous honors for her legal and human rights work. The Committee to Protect Journalists presented her with its Gwen Ifill Press Freedom Award in 2020, recognizing her work defending journalists. She has also received honors from the American Society of International Law, the Reporters Committee for Freedom of the Press, the Simon Wiesenthal Center, and other organizations.
""Time"" named Clooney one of its Women of the Year in 2022. In 2024, she received the Legal 500 award for International Law Junior of the Year. Professional legal directories including Chambers and Partners and Legal 500 have repeatedly ranked her among Britain's leading practitioners in international human rights law and international criminal law.
Amal met George Clooney in 2013 after being introduced through a mutual friend. They became engaged the following year and married in September 2014 in Venice, Italy. The wedding attracted enormous international media attention, in part because George had previously been one of Hollywood's most famous longtime bachelors.
In June 2017, Amal gave birth to twins, Ella and Alexander. George has frequently credited Amal and their children with dramatically changing his personal life and priorities. The couple divide their time among properties in Europe and the United States while also working together through the Clooney Foundation for Justice.
George and Amal own an extensive international real estate portfolio. One of their best-known properties is Villa Oleandra, George's villa in Laglio on Italy's Lake Como, which he purchased years before meeting Amal. The estate has become closely associated with the couple and has hosted numerous prominent friends and guests.
After marrying, the Clooneys purchased a historic estate on an island in the River Thames in Berkshire, England, for approximately $13 million and completed a major renovation. They also acquired a luxury condominium in Manhattan for approximately $14.7 million, providing Amal with a base near New York's legal and academic institutions. In 2021, the couple added Domaine du Canadel, a large estate in Provence, France, reportedly paying approximately $8 million for the property.
George previously owned a custom-built property in Los Cabos, Mexico, next door to close friends Rande Gerber and Cindy Crawford . The two neighboring homes were sold together in 2016 for a reported $100 million. The Clooneys also maintained George's longtime Studio City residence in Los Angeles for years before selling the property in 2024 for $14.5 million.",ok
