9380224	880370996812951553	880399462627241984	880410634495676416	880417152271962112	880425079187709953	880432968598257665	880456012343504896	880493155753353217	880493157603041280	880545586939740160	880548250859835393	880637831118454785	880703052742832129	880797575451901952	880798830316445697	880800344275603456	880801364858851328	880803416884301826	880804874551775238	880806955832496130	880807389456457729	880810406851334149	880826012724015109	880826771763073024	880827269756985344	880828527926530048	880829570835939328	880833829002412036	880858530772865024	880882886618042373	880886170871357440	880888672031322112	880898504922628096	880901343086604290	880913096642088960	881151657760890880	881152509427036162	881245201804648448	881599201351806982,0,celebritynetworth.com,23,"Jason Statham is a British actor and producer who has a net worth of $150 million.
Jason Statham has become one of the most commercially successful action stars of his generation, building a career around an unusually convincing combination of athleticism, martial arts, stunt work, and dry British humor. Before acting, Statham spent more than a decade competing as a diver for Britain's national squad and represented England at the 1990 Commonwealth Games. He later supported himself selling goods on London streets and worked as a model before filmmaker Guy Ritchie cast him in 1998's ""Lock, Stock and Two Smoking Barrels.""
Statham followed that breakthrough with ""Snatch,"" ""The Italian Job,"" and the ""Transporter"" trilogy, which established the persona that would define much of his career: highly skilled, physically imposing, laconic, and usually surrounded by cars, guns, and elaborate fight choreography. He went on to headline or co-star in the ""Crank,"" ""Expendables,"" ""Fast & Furious,"" ""Mechanic,"" and ""Meg"" franchises, while also showing a gift for comedy opposite Melissa McCarthy in ""Spy.""
His films as a leading actor have generated more than $6.4 billion at the worldwide box office. Some of his largest commercial successes include ""Furious 7,"" ""The Fate of the Furious,"" ""Fast & Furious Presents: Hobbs & Shaw,"" ""The Meg,"" and ""Meg 2: The Trench."" Statham earned $13 million for ""Hobbs & Shaw."" In the 2020s, he also moved more aggressively into producing his own starring vehicles, including ""The Beekeeper,"" ""A Working Man,"" ""Shelter,"" and ""Mutiny.""
Jason Statham was born on July 26, 1967, in Shirebrook, Derbyshire, England. He grew up largely in Great Yarmouth. His mother, Eileen, was a dancer, while his father, Barry, worked as a street merchant and also performed as a lounge singer.
Athletics were an important part of Jason's childhood. His father had experience in boxing and gymnastics, and Statham began developing the strength and coordination that would eventually become central to his movie career.
His first serious ambition was diving. Statham joined the British national diving squad and spent roughly 12 years competing at a high level. Contrary to a claim that has appeared in some biographies, he did not compete in the Olympic Games.
He did, however, represent England at the 1990 Commonwealth Games in Auckland, New Zealand. Statham competed in three events, finishing eighth in the 1-meter springboard and 11th in both the 3-meter springboard and 10-meter platform. He later finished 12th in the platform event at the 1992 World Championships.
Diving was an amateur pursuit and did not provide much of an income. To support himself, Statham began working the streets of London, selling perfume, jewelry, and other merchandise. He became extremely skilled at the fast-talking sales pitch required to convince strangers that they were getting an expensive product at a bargain price.
That seemingly unrelated job would eventually lead directly to his movie career.
Statham eventually moved into modeling, appearing in campaigns for companies including French Connection and Levi's. His relationship with French Connection helped put him in contact with a young filmmaker named Guy Ritchie, who was preparing his first feature film.
Ritchie was casting a streetwise con artist named Bacon in ""Lock, Stock and Two Smoking Barrels."" When he learned that Statham had actually spent years selling questionable merchandise on London streets, he effectively asked Jason to recreate his real-life sales routine during the audition. Statham reportedly convinced Ritchie to buy imitation jewelry and then refused to give the director his money back.
Released in 1998, ""Lock, Stock and Two Smoking Barrels"" became an international success and turned both Ritchie and Statham into major new names in British cinema. Jason had never attended drama school and had essentially been cast because he could authentically perform a version of the life he had already lived.
Ritchie brought him back for 2000's ""Snatch."" Statham played Turkish, an underground boxing promoter who becomes entangled with gangsters and an unpredictable bare-knuckle fighter played by Brad Pitt . ""Snatch"" became an even larger success and established that Statham's first performance had not been a fluke.
After ""Snatch,"" Statham began appearing in Hollywood productions including ""Ghosts of Mars"" and ""The One."" His career changed again when he was cast as professional driver and mercenary Frank Martin in 2002's ""The Transporter.""
The role took advantage of virtually everything that made Statham distinctive. He was athletic enough to perform substantial amounts of his own fight and stunt work, had experience with martial arts, could credibly drive and handle weapons on screen, and possessed the detached charisma required for a character who lived by a strict professional code.
""The Transporter"" became a hit and generated sequels in 2005 and 2008. More importantly, Frank Martin established Statham as a bankable action lead at a time when relatively few British actors were headlining old-school martial-arts-heavy action movies.
Between ""Transporter"" films, Jason appeared in the 2003 remake of ""The Italian Job"" alongside Mark Wahlberg , Charlize Theron , and Edward Norton . He also reunited with Guy Ritchie for 2005's ""Revolver.""
Statham expanded his action résumé with 2006's ""Crank,"" playing hitman Chev Chelios, who must keep his adrenaline elevated to survive being poisoned. The deliberately outrageous movie became a cult hit and produced the 2009 sequel ""Crank: High Voltage.""
He followed with films including ""War,"" ""The Bank Job,"" and ""Death Race."" ""The Bank Job,"" in particular, gave Statham a somewhat more grounded crime role and received stronger reviews than many of his action vehicles.
In 2010, Statham joined Sylvester Stallone in ""The Expendables,"" an ensemble designed around multiple generations of action stars. Jason played knife specialist Lee Christmas alongside a cast that included Stallone, Jet Li , Dolph Lundgren , Mickey Rourke , and Terry Crews .
The film earned more than $270 million worldwide and launched another franchise for Statham. He returned for ""The Expendables 2"" in 2012 and ""The Expendables 3"" in 2014.
When the series returned with ""Expend4bles"" in 2023, Statham's Lee Christmas moved into an even more central role. The fourth installment was a major box-office disappointment, but by that point the franchise had already cemented Statham's place alongside many of the action stars who had dominated the genre before him.
Statham starred as assassin Arthur Bishop in 2011's ""The Mechanic,"" a remake of the 1972 Charles Bronson film. He returned for 2016's ""Mechanic: Resurrection,"" which proved substantially more successful internationally, earning more than $125 million worldwide.
Other films during this period included ""Blitz,"" ""Killer Elite,"" ""Safe,"" ""Parker,"" ""Homefront,"" and ""Wild Card."" Although many of these movies occupied a similar action-thriller lane, Statham developed an unusually dependable international audience.
One of the biggest departures from his standard persona came with Paul Feig's 2015 comedy ""Spy."" Playing wildly overconfident intelligence agent Rick Ford, Statham essentially parodied the indestructible characters he had spent years playing.
Rather than changing his usual dead-serious delivery, he used it to make increasingly absurd claims about his exploits. The performance earned him a Critics Choice Award nomination for Best Actor in a Comedy and demonstrated a comedic ability that his earlier films had only occasionally exploited.
Statham entered one of the biggest movie franchises in history with an uncredited appearance at the end of 2013's ""Fast & Furious 6."" The scene revealed that his character, Deckard Shaw, had been responsible for the death of Han, setting him up as the primary villain of ""Furious 7.""
Released in 2015, ""Furious 7"" became a global phenomenon, earning more than $1.5 billion worldwide. Statham's Shaw spent much of the movie battling characters played by Vin Diesel and Dwayne Johnson .
Deckard Shaw gradually shifted from villain to antihero. Statham returned for 2017's ""The Fate of the Furious,"" which earned more than $1.2 billion worldwide, and his antagonistic chemistry with Dwayne ""The Rock"" Johnson became one of the movie's most popular elements.
That pairing led to the 2019 spin-off ""Fast & Furious Presents: Hobbs & Shaw."" Statham and Johnson starred as reluctant partners forced to stop an enhanced terrorist played by Idris Elba . The film earned $760.7 million worldwide.
Statham received a $13 million salary for ""Hobbs & Shaw,"" placing him among Hollywood's highest-paid actors at the time. Johnson received $20 million, while Elba earned $8 million.
Jason later appeared in ""F9"" and returned again as Deckard Shaw in 2023's ""Fast X.""
Statham launched another major franchise with 2018's ""The Meg."" He starred as rescue diver Jonas Taylor, who is recruited to confront a giant prehistoric shark known as a megalodon.
The premise was unapologetically ridiculous, but the film's international appeal was enormous. ""The Meg"" earned $529.3 million worldwide against a $130 million production budget, making it one of the largest standalone box-office successes of Statham's career.
He returned for 2023's ""Meg 2: The Trench."" The sequel earned nearly $400 million worldwide, pushing the two-film franchise past $900 million in theatrical revenue.
More than two decades after ""Lock, Stock and Two Smoking Barrels"" launched both of their careers, Statham and Guy Ritchie reunited for 2021's ""Wrath of Man."" Jason played a mysterious armored-truck employee seeking revenge following the murder of his son.
The movie earned more than $100 million worldwide despite being released during the pandemic-era recovery of movie theaters. It also marked the beginning of a new run of collaborations between Statham and Ritchie.
They worked together again on ""Operation Fortune: Ruse de Guerre,"" with Statham playing elite intelligence operative Orson Fortune opposite Aubrey Plaza, Josh Hartnett , Cary Elwes , and Hugh Grant .
As Statham moved through his 50s, his action-star career showed remarkably little sign of slowing down. He also began taking greater control behind the scenes through his production company, Punch Palace Productions.
In 2024, Statham starred in and produced David Ayer's ""The Beekeeper."" He played Adam Clay, a former operative who launches a violent campaign against a cybercrime organization after a close friend is financially devastated by a scam.
Made for about $40 million, ""The Beekeeper"" earned $162.6 million worldwide. The result demonstrated that Statham could still open an original R-rated action movie decades after his debut.
He continued working with Ayer on 2025's ""A Working Man."" The screenplay was co-written by Sylvester Stallone and adapted from Chuck Dixon's novel ""Levon's Trade."" Statham starred as former black-ops soldier Levon Cade and also served as a producer. The film earned $98.6 million worldwide on a $40 million budget.
Statham continued the producer-star model with 2026's ""Shelter,"" directed by Ric Roman Waugh, and ""Mutiny,"" directed by Jean-François Richet. On ""Mutiny,"" Punch Palace was one of the production companies and Statham again received a producer credit.
By this stage of his career, he had evolved from an actor hired to play tough guys into a producer capable of developing action vehicles specifically around his screen persona.
Statham's career has been unusually valuable because of his consistency. His leading-role filmography has generated more than $6.4 billion worldwide, and that figure does not depend on one single franchise.
He has had major commercial success with ""Fast & Furious,"" ""The Meg,"" ""The Expendables,"" ""Transporter,"" ""The Mechanic,"" and standalone films including ""The Beekeeper."" His strongest publicly documented individual salary is the $13 million he received for 2019's ""Fast & Furious Presents: Hobbs & Shaw.""
Claims online that Statham routinely receives $20 million to $25 million per movie are much less well documented. What is clear is that his earning power has expanded beyond a conventional acting fee as he has increasingly taken producer credits and worked through Punch Palace Productions.
Statham's athletic background has always been central to his movie career. In addition to his years as an elite diver, he has trained extensively in disciplines including boxing, kickboxing, judo, Brazilian jiu-jitsu, and other martial arts.
He performs a significant amount of his own physical work on screen and has long pushed for stunt professionals to receive greater recognition within the film industry. In 2015, Statham publicly argued that stunt performers deserved their own Academy Award category, noting that many performers risk their lives without receiving the recognition afforded to other filmmaking disciplines.
A decade later, the Academy of Motion Picture Arts and Sciences announced the creation of an Oscar for Achievement in Stunt Design. The award begins with films released in 2027.
Statham began dating English model and actress Rosie Huntington-Whiteley in 2010. They became engaged in 2016.
The couple welcomed their son, Jack, in 2017 and their daughter, Isabella, in 2022. Despite their long engagement, Jason and Rosie have generally kept their family life private and have not treated marriage as a prerequisite to building their life together.
Statham remains intensely committed to physical training even when he is not preparing for a film. His workouts combine strength training, gymnastics, martial arts, swimming, and functional conditioning rather than traditional bodybuilding.
Jason Statham has completed a number of significant Los Angeles real estate deals and has shown a particular interest in renovating architecturally distinctive homes.
In 2005, Jason paid $2.4 million for a 1957 post-and-beam house in the Hollywood Hills. The three-bedroom property featured walls of glass opening to a swimming pool. He sold the home in 2011 for $2.7 million.
In 2009, Statham paid $10.625 million for an oceanfront home inside the exclusive gated Malibu Colony community. He extensively renovated the approximately 3,900-square-foot property, which included four bedrooms, an oceanfront deck, guesthouse, outdoor shower, and spa. Jason and Rosie listed the home for $19.95 million in 2019 and sold it in January 2020 for $18.5 million.
In 2011, Jason paid $7.325 million for a Hollywood Hills compound previously owned by Ben Stiller . The half-acre estate centered around a restored 1929 Mediterranean-style residence with six bedrooms, a library, gym, office, guesthouse, courtyards, and swimming pool. Statham and Rosie listed it for $8.999 million in 2015 and sold it shortly afterward for $9.2 million to ""The Big Bang Theory"" star Johnny Galecki .
Jason was particularly active in the Los Angeles property market in 2015. In April of that year, he and Rosie paid $12.995 million for a contemporary mansion in Beverly Hills that had been extensively renovated by architect Jeffrey Allsbrook. The roughly 5,600-square-foot home had five bedrooms, eight bathrooms, a media room, multiple fireplaces, a koi pond, and extensive privacy landscaping.
They used the Beverly Hills property as a primary Los Angeles residence for nearly a decade. In early 2025, they listed it for $19.95 million and quickly found a buyer. The sale closed in February 2025 for $19.9 million, nearly $7 million more than their original purchase price before accounting for renovations, commissions, taxes, and other ownership costs.
Just one month after buying the Beverly Hills house in 2015, Statham paid another $2.7 million for a Midcentury Modern home in the Hollywood Hills above the Sunset Strip. He undertook an 18-month renovation with Jeffrey Allsbrook and Standard Architecture, transforming the approximately 3,000-square-foot property with Scandinavian-inspired interiors, cedar ceilings, polished concrete floors, a gym, sauna, ice bath, and saltwater swimming pool.
Jason initially envisioned that property as a combination guesthouse, office, and workout space rather than his principal residence. He listed it for $6.995 million in 2020 and later reduced the asking price to $6.2 million.",ok
gossipcop-915402,https://www.express.co.uk/life-style/life/922441/cheryl-liam-payne-brits-body-language,Cheryl & Liam Payne BRITs ‘safe word’ moment shows THIS according to body language expert,966435156478263296	966435373961244672	966435734709309440	966437011727904768	966437170545242112	966443373770035200	966443941104033793	966445049339838464	966475860701253632	966486843322785792	966711832005181440,0,express.co.uk,89,"Cheryl , 34, and Liam Payne, 24, attended the BRIT Awards last night, leaving young baby Bear at home for a well-deserved night off.
The couple found themselves in the spotlight after host Jack Whitehall stopped by their table for a chat.
Things got steamy after Jack asked Liam if there was a “safe word” for his Fifty Shades of Grey performance with Rita Ora, which was coming later in the show.
Liam joined in the joke and pointing at Cheryl, said: “She knows.”
But instead of playing coy, Cheryl shared their bedroom secret, and jokingly said their safe word is “don’t stop” - to the shock and amusement of the crowd.
The clip has since gone viral - but what does a body language expert say about the joke?
Expert Judi James has watched the footage and shared her thoughts with Express.co.uk.
She said: ""Probably the last thing you want when you're in the middle of rumours of a crisis in your relationship is Jack Whitehall thrusting a mic under your already embarrassed-looking guy's nose and cracking jokes about date nights.
Cheryl looked alert from the start with her upright back and fiddly hand clasp on the table
""Cheryl looked alert from the start with her upright back and fiddly hand clasp on the table suggesting she might have already smelled comedy trouble brewing.
""Her smile looked fixed and signs of anxiety were suggested in the way she started picking gently at her own fingers.
""When she dropped one hand, the other hand picked away at itself in what is known as body language leakage.
""This is when a smile is outweighed by signs of anxiety or discomfort from the rest of the body, especially the hands or the feet.
""As the chat went on Cheryl’s clasped hand raised into a pose I always call 'hamster hands'. Many female presenters do it and it involves the hands being clasped at rib or chest height and only loosely joined at the fingers, with the wrists bent. It's a self-protective gesture of vulnerability but it can also announce a polite desire to speak or take over when used on TV.
""Liam's response to the 50 Shades question was to evaporate into awkwardness, first leaning away from his host and then leaning away from Cheryl once she made her ‘safe word’ joke, with a look of schoolboy mock-horror on his face and patting one hand on his chest in what looked like a protective gesture.
""He then ran his hand across his own face in a cut-off ritual and Cheryl’s cupping face-pat response at the end looked like a fond reward and reassurance for any embarrassment.”
