gossipcop-9460765464,www.billboard.com/articles/news/8096593/miley-cyrus-lady-gaga-targeted-by-la-street-artist-ahead-of-grammys,"Miley Cyrus, Lady Gaga Targeted by L.A. Street Artist Ahead of Grammys",957300583957807104	957303715639173120	957305477079035905	957309243652059136	957315413213200384	957320073110110208	957322204445687809	957325497473449984	957361589270671360	957365474278494209	957368256423018497	957368884482359297	957369638366494720	957371662978113536	957373172747137024	957384744907362309	957403608349278208	957403610765254657	957498733670928384	957524909730693120	957527946293661696	957572980535566339	960608777451032576	960608798779084805	962943631106732033	968306711646887939	968306714746458112,1,billboard.com,70,"Nude images of Miley Cyrus and Lady Gaga are being used in street art to attack the music industry a day ahead of the Grammys.
The perpetrator, a conservative provocateur who goes by the pseudonym Sabo, used previously published photos of Cyrus and Gaga that he slapped onto large posters that appear at first glance to be advertisements for the 60th Annual Grammy Awards, set for telecast Sunday night on CBS from Madison Square Garden in New York.
On closer inspection, however, the posters are far more pornographic than would be allowed by any true ad, and The Hollywood Reporter blocked out portions of the images in the photos of the posters here .
Related LL Cool J Finally Explains His Viral WWE Emote From the Early 2000s: 'I Do What I Wanna Do. I'll Own It.' Angel Diaz
The faux ads feature the logos of the network and the Grammys, one with a nude Gaga and another with a naked Cyrus, and each poster has the text: #WeAllKnew, a reference to the spate of allegations against powerful men in media who have harassed women, and to those who knew of the harassment but said nothing.
Some of the posters also include the text: “Long Live Music.”
The photos Sabo used of Cyrus were taken by Terry Richardson, who has been accused of sexually harassing several models.
Sabo hung his posters before the sun rose on Saturday (Jan. 27) in several difficult-to-reach places in Hollywood, including behind locked, glass cases at bus stops where legitimate advertisements usually reside.
Contacted on Saturday, Sabo told THR he targeted the music industry because of what he considers misogynistic lyrics in rap and hip hop.
“How can anyone be upset with me putting these up when no one seems upset about what the music industry pushes to our kids?” he asked.
This article originally appeared on The Hollywood Reporter .
