gossipcop-8941881759,www.smh.com.au/entertainment/movies/jennifer-lawrence-bill-murray-johnny-depp-and-the-role-of-the-muse-20151221-glsm3u.html,"Jennifer Lawrence, Bill Murray, Johnny Depp and the role of the muse",680373185552879616	680384550522900480	680397585849294848	680409797183709184	680417071247667200	963909259158310913	963925342053326849	963987691678318592	964016505460584448	964016632690601984	964102034080321537	964130573206908931	964137173938302977	964153400425410560	964424456369160192	964534058276573185	967937347894603777,1,smh.com.au,68,"Jennifer Lawrence said a peculiar thing the other day while talking about Joy , her new film for the notably ""difficult"" director David O. Russell. ""David respects me so much that he will literally forget what sex I am,"" she said with a laugh. ""He sometimes refers to me as a 'he' without realising it."" For anyone interested in gender identity, there is a can of worms right there: even for the charmingly feisty Lawrence, apparently, real acceptance means being counted as one of the boys.
At the same time, Lawrence's honorary gender neutrality invites some reflections on the working partnerships between directors and actors, which can last through several films or even entire careers. This does not, necessarily, mean becoming one of the boys. Some actors – traditionally women – are recognised as muses, inspiring the male directors who construct scenarios around them and explore their faces with the camera. Many muses have become partners or wives: Ingrid Bergman with Roberto Rossellini, Anna Karina and Jean-Luc Godard and Gena Rowlands with John Cassavetes spring to mind, but the list is a long one.
Indeed, the muse model is so pervasive that it has become one of the defining ways we read films. Years ago, I interviewed the French director Leos Carax about the film Les Amants du Pont-Neuf , which starred his then lover Juliette Binoche. As a student, he readily admitted, he would go to the tiny cinemas on the Left Bank to screenings of old movies by Fellini (whose wife Giulietta Masina was his favoured leading lady) or Antonioni (Monica Vitti) and dream of someday directing a film and having a beautiful girlfriend: for him, the work and the woman were so intertwined as to be synonymous. He had then been with Binoche for three years; I wasn't to know they were going through the throes of splitting up. What was it like to work with someone he knew so well? ""I do not think,"" he spat, ""that I know her well at all.""
Such is the ambivalence of the artist-muse relationship: it can be scratchy, even explosive. But even in a relationship as clearly collaborative as that between German director Christian Petzold and the actor Nina Hoss, who have made six films together as platonic friends, there still is a gap of understanding. Hoss believes that difference is a source of strength. ""Even though we are friends, I always have the feeling that Christian thinks he doesn't quite understand me – and I like that,"" she told me in the course of discussing their new film Phoenix . ""I think the ideas he puts into his characters have a lot to do with him, which may be why he always chooses women. It gives him some distance, which keeps it alive and interesting. And I think I am able to surprise him.""
Danish director Lars von Trier also focuses on female protagonists – played by Emily Watson in Breaking the Waves , Bjork in Dancer in the Dark , Nicole Kidman in Dogville , and Charlotte Gainsbourg in Nymphomania , among others – whom he is regularly accused of torturing or turning into victims. Just as regularly, he insists all these characters are him. ""My technique is that I divide my personality into the characters that I write,"" he said in an interview about Melancholia , which featured Kirsten Dunst as a depressive, ""and then, very early on, they get a life of their own"".
The actor as the director's avatar, the ""I"" figure who bears the story's emotional weight: this is actually a much more frequent pairing than maker with muse, but mainly between men. John Ford and John Wayne were a classic double act; Judd Apatow and Seth Rogen (or Jonah Hill or Paul Rudd – any of Apatow's friends, essentially) is a current example. Over long careers, directors Martin Scorsese and Ron Howard have had serial connections with actors of different ages – Robert de Niro and Leonardo DiCaprio are Scorsese's boys, while Howard says he ""is always looking for things to do"" with Tom Hanks, Russell Crowe and now Chris Hemsworth.
Apatow himself recognised how the buddy system kept women out of leading roles, specifically out of comic ones. ""The reality is, I'm a dude,"" he said once, ""and I understand the dude thing, so I lean men just the way Spike Lee leans African-American."" He subsequently became a moving force in bringing women's humour to the screen, producing Bridesmaids and recently directing Amy Schumer's script for Trainwreck . Not that these dyads are a dude thing. Women work similarly with women: writer and director Nora Ephron found the perfect embodiment of her soft screwball-comic style in Meg Ryan, for example. There just aren't so many examples.
Anyway, none of these relationships is definitive; in the end, anyone can be anything. Johnny Depp is so perfectly attuned to Tim Burton's weird fancies that he is at once both muse and avatar, Burton's inspiration and his perfect instrument. When Depp and Burton first met, they found they had read the same books as children, been scared by the same obscure horror film and were linked by ""a kind of suburban white-trashy connective strand"", as Burton put it in a joint interview with Esquire . ""We'll cross-reference things that wouldn't really make sense to the normal person."" When he made the musical Sweeney Todd , he cast Depp without even asking whether he could sing. Burton's former partner Helena Bonham Carter, by contrast, had to audition for every film they made together.
Directors may also work with loose companies of actors they carry from one film to the next like theatrical repertory groups. Quentin Tarantino, Paul Thomas Anderson and Steven Soderbergh are good examples, although the daddy in this respect is Wes Anderson, who goes one better by endeavouring to make all his films in single locations where actors both work and live together. His most stalwart regular is Bill Murray; they have made seven films together. As the troupe's oldest member, Murray is less like an avatar than an irascible but beloved uncle who must be included in every family do; Anderson calls him his lucky charm.
Russell works repeatedly with actors too, but a bit differently. For a start, his central working partnership is with a screen couple: Jennifer Lawrence and Bradley Cooper, who have appeared together in Silver Linings Playbook , American Hustle and now Joy, as well as appearing as a troubled married couple in Danish director Susanna Bier's Serena . Joy takes its title from Lawrence's character, based on the real Joy Mangano, who invented the self-wringing mop. Mangano rose from mortgaged penury to become the queen of a household gadgets empire; Lawrence plays her with her customary zest. Cooper plays the relatively minor part of the television executive who first featured her on a cable shopping channel.
When they are both on screen, however, Russell's focus is less on either character than on the sparky dynamic between them. In Silver Linings that connection was romantic; in Hustle it was overtly and somewhat creepily sexual; in this film it is comradely but spiked with flirtation, a certain professional condescension on Cooper's part and ultimately with a spirit of competition. Robert de Niro plays support act, as he has in their previous films; as Mangano's father he both denigrates his daughter and depends on her. Russell is estranged from his own Italian mother and Jewish father; his films usually give families a bad rap.
But then, as previously noted, he's difficult. Famously, he got into a fistfight with George Clooney on the set of Three Kings ; just as famously, thanks to the internet, he had a screaming match with Lily Tomlin while making I Heart Huckabees ; the more recent, bigger budget film sets have sprung fewer leaks, but there was a story flying around during Joy's filming of a flaming row between Russell and Lawrence. Lawrence went online – purportedly, anyway – in a carefully worded defence of her director.  ""David O. Russell is one of my closest friends and we have an amazing collaborative working relationship. I adore this man and he does not deserve this tabloid malarkey,"" she wrote on a fansite. ""This movie is going great and I'm having a blast making it!""
Fine, but – note well – not actually a denial that the fight happened; given Russell's previous form, my money would be where his mouth is. Usually, these relationships can be seen to bring an ease to the process of filmmaking, where actor and director share a shorthand, but that isn't necessarily true. Look at Werner Herzog who, a few years after his favourite actor Klaus Kinski died, he made a documentary about their relationship called My Best Fiend . ""People think we had a love-hate relationship,"" Herzog once said. ""Well, I did not love him, nor did I hate him. We had mutual respect for each other, even as we both planned each other's murder."" He also said Kinski was one of the few people from whom he learned anything. Teacher, would-be murderer and the visual manifestation of all Herzog's darkest imaginings: you can't ask more of a muse than that.
