457	950701351780081664	951238676654252032	951348313802006528	953323531370160128	953698625200246784	953813584080973824	954794059314458624	954794072211935233	955339074407206914	955946498751193088	956133710860038144	956429016805924864	957203128440508416	957900660258869249	961990873826263040	962134031897939969	962134035270127616	962393349742350336	962695827658625024	963431315105308673	964862100110798849	967332086997712897	968976855675080705	969098000290152449	970542242585772032	970595668761903104	971083024636039169	971203270126452736	978544681763078144	978545212510240769	983394319527690245	987666067093733376	987666096395243520	1000640000789303296	1006914153821323266	1014127924545556480,1,celebritynetworth.com,23,"Margot Robbie is an Australian actress and producer who has a net worth of $60 million. Margot Robbie first gained recognition on the Australian soap opera ""Neighbours"" before moving to Hollywood and delivering her breakthrough performance in Martin Scorsese's ""The Wolf of Wall Street."" She subsequently became one of the industry's most bankable actresses through films including ""Suicide Squad,"" ""I, Tonya,"" ""Once Upon a Time in Hollywood,"" ""Bombshell,"" and ""Barbie.""
Robbie has earned three Academy Award nominations: Best Actress for ""I, Tonya,"" Best Supporting Actress for ""Bombshell,"" and Best Picture as a producer of ""Barbie."" Her portrayal of Harley Quinn in ""Suicide Squad,"" ""Birds of Prey,"" and ""The Suicide Squad"" also turned her into one of the defining stars of the modern superhero era.
An increasingly important source of Robbie's fortune is LuckyChap Entertainment, the production company she co-founded with her husband, Tom Ackerley , and their business partners. LuckyChap has produced films and television programs including ""I, Tonya,"" ""Promising Young Woman,"" ""Maid,"" ""Saltburn,"" and ""Barbie."" Robbie earned more than $50 million from her acting salary and profit participation on ""Barbie,"" making it one of the largest single-film paydays ever received by an actress.
Between September 2018 and September 2019, Margot earned approximately $24 million from acting, producing, and endorsement deals. That was enough to make her one of the highest-paid actresses in the world during that period.
Her earnings increased significantly following the success of ""Barbie."" Robbie received an upfront salary of approximately $12.5 million to star in the film. Because she also produced the movie through LuckyChap and negotiated a share of its financial success, her eventual compensation was expected to exceed $50 million.
Robbie spent years attempting to bring a live-action Barbie movie to the screen. After acquiring the project and developing it through LuckyChap, she recruited Greta Gerwig to direct and co-write the film with Noah Baumbach .
Robbie starred as the central version of Barbie opposite Ryan Gosling as Ken. Released in 2023, ""Barbie"" became a global cultural phenomenon and earned more than $1.4 billion at the worldwide box office. It became Warner Bros.' highest-grossing film and the highest-grossing movie directed solely by a woman.
Robbie's financial participation went far beyond a traditional acting salary. As both the star and a producer, she earned more than $50 million from her upfront compensation, producing fees, bonuses, and backend participation.
Robbie also received an Academy Award nomination for Best Picture as one of the film's producers. Although she was not nominated in the acting category, her performance earned Golden Globe and BAFTA nominations.
In 2026, Warner Bros. began attempting to negotiate a sequel with Robbie, Gerwig, and Gosling. Because the principal talent did not agree to sequel terms when making the original film, each would need to negotiate an entirely new contract. The proposed offers included upfront raises and profit participation, but no completed agreement was announced.
Margot Elise Robbie was born on July 2, 1990, in Dalby, Queensland, Australia. She grew up on the Gold Coast with her mother, Sarie Kessler, and three siblings. The family spent considerable time on her grandparents' farm.
Robbie attended Somerset College, where she studied drama. As a teenager, she worked several jobs simultaneously, including positions at a Subway restaurant, a surf shop, and a cleaning business.
At age 17, Robbie moved to Melbourne to pursue acting. She began appearing in commercials and low-budget independent films before securing guest roles on Australian television programs.
Robbie's breakthrough in Australia came in 2008 when she was cast as Donna Freedman on the long-running soap opera ""Neighbours."" The role was initially intended to be temporary, but producers expanded it after responding positively to her performance.
Robbie remained with the series until 2011 and received two Logie Award nominations. The exposure gave her a national profile in Australia and provided the steady acting experience she needed to pursue work internationally.
After leaving ""Neighbours,"" Robbie moved to Los Angeles. She landed a role on the ABC period drama ""Pan Am,"" which followed the pilots and flight attendants of the famous airline during the 1960s. The series lasted one season but introduced Robbie to American audiences.
Robbie appeared in the romantic comedy ""About Time"" before securing her major Hollywood breakthrough in 2013.
She was cast as Naomi Lapaglia in Martin Scorsese's ""The Wolf of Wall Street."" Robbie starred opposite Leonardo DiCaprio , who played disgraced stockbroker Jordan Belfort .
The film earned nearly $400 million worldwide, and Robbie's performance attracted substantial attention. Her ability to hold her own opposite DiCaprio established her as one of Hollywood's fastest-rising actresses.
She followed the film with roles in ""Suite Française,"" ""Z for Zachariah,"" ""Focus,"" and ""Whiskey Tango Foxtrot."" She also appeared as herself in the financial satire ""The Big Short.""
Robbie reached a new level of international fame when she was cast as Harley Quinn in the 2016 superhero film ""Suicide Squad.""
Although the movie received largely negative reviews, Robbie's performance was widely considered its highlight. Her version of Harley Quinn became a pop-culture phenomenon, inspiring merchandise, costumes, and additional films centered on the character.
Robbie reprised the role in 2020's ""Birds of Prey,"" which she also produced through LuckyChap. She played Harley for a third time in James Gunn's 2021 film ""The Suicide Squad.""
The character gave Robbie a major commercial franchise while also demonstrating the value of controlling projects as a producer. Rather than waiting for a studio to determine Harley Quinn's future, Robbie helped develop ""Birds of Prey"" and assembled its largely female creative team.
In 2017, Robbie produced and starred in ""I, Tonya,"" a darkly comedic account of figure skater Tonya Harding and the scandal surrounding the attack on Nancy Kerrigan .
Robbie's performance earned her first Academy Award nomination for Best Actress, along with Golden Globe, BAFTA, and Screen Actors Guild Award nominations. Allison Janney won the Academy Award for Best Supporting Actress for playing Harding's mother.
The success of ""I, Tonya"" was also an important validation of LuckyChap. It proved that Robbie could use her commercial profile to develop unconventional projects that major studios might otherwise overlook.
Robbie subsequently portrayed Queen Elizabeth I in ""Mary Queen of Scots,"" earning another BAFTA nomination. In 2019, she played Sharon Tate in Quentin Tarantino's ""Once Upon a Time in Hollywood.""
That same year, Robbie appeared alongside Charlize Theron and Nicole Kidman in ""Bombshell."" Her performance as the fictional Fox News employee Kayla Pospisil earned her an Academy Award nomination for Best Supporting Actress.
Robbie co-founded LuckyChap Entertainment in 2014 with Tom Ackerley, Josey McNamara, and Sophia Kerr. The company was created partly to give Robbie greater control over her career and partly to develop projects focused on women both in front of and behind the camera.
LuckyChap's first major critical breakthrough was ""I, Tonya."" The company later produced the television series ""Dollface"" and ""Maid,"" along with the films ""Birds of Prey,"" ""Promising Young Woman,"" ""Barbie,"" ""Saltburn,"" and ""My Old Ass.""
""Promising Young Woman"" earned five Academy Award nominations and won Best Original Screenplay for writer and director Emerald Fennell. The Netflix limited series ""Maid"" became one of the streaming service's most-watched programs and received multiple Emmy nominations.
LuckyChap reunited with Fennell for ""Saltburn,"" which became one of the most discussed films of 2023 after finding a large audience through streaming and social media.
The success of LuckyChap is an important component of Robbie's net worth. In addition to collecting acting salaries, she can earn producer fees and potentially participate in the long-term profits generated by the company's movies, television programs, remake rights, and licensing agreements.
Robbie has earned millions of dollars through endorsement and brand-ambassador agreements.
In 2016, she became the face of Calvin Klein's ""Deep Euphoria"" fragrance. She subsequently appeared in a global campaign for Nissan's electric vehicles.
In 2018, Robbie began a major relationship with Chanel. She has represented the company in fashion and beauty campaigns and became the face of its ""Gabrielle Essence"" fragrance.
Her endorsement income has helped make Robbie one of the highest-paid actresses in the world even during years when she released relatively few films.
Robbie met British assistant director and producer Tom Ackerley while working on the 2014 film ""Suite Française."" They began dating and married in a private ceremony in Byron Bay, Australia, in December 2016.
Ackerley is also one of Robbie's partners in LuckyChap Entertainment. The couple initially lived with several friends and business partners in London before relocating to Los Angeles.
Robbie has generally kept her private life separate from her public career.
In 2017, Robbie paid approximately $2.73 million for a home in Los Angeles' Hancock Park neighborhood. The property featured a swimming pool, wine cellar, and private guest accommodations.
In 2018, she paid approximately $950,000 for another Los Angeles home. She listed that property for around $1.2 million in 2020.
Robbie has also invested in property in Australia. She paid approximately $1.8 million for a gated duplex on the Gold Coast, providing the homes to members of her family. Earlier in her career, she paid off her mother's mortgage as a birthday gift.
In 2019, Robbie and Ackerley paid approximately $5 million for a private compound in Venice, California. The property included multiple structures, outdoor space, and a swimming pool.",ok
gossipcop-168175528,www.thehollywoodgossip.com/2018/01/mandy-teefey-speaks-out-mother-of-selena-gomez-wants-to-quash-fe/,"Mandy Teefey Speaks Out; Selena Gomez, Mom Look to Quash Beef Rumors",953306037435752448	953307169390301187	953307250722041858	953307589202374656	953307635205398528	953308128321462284	953310701656920064	953316177954201607	953326424114515968	953326430322135040,1,thehollywoodgossip.com,68,"Selena Gomez is back with Justin Bieber.
Plenty of people aren’t thrilled with her choice, but her mom Mandy Teefey got so upset that she had a breakdown and was hospitalized over it.
Nobody worries like your mom worries, folks.
But now, Mandy Teefey is opening up and setting the record straight about some of the wild stories about her feud with Selena.
Selena Gomez has been through so, so much.
She’s waging an uphill battle against Lupus that, at times, seems to rob her of her strength and her ability to live her life.
At present, her rumored feud with her mother doesn’t seem to be doing her any favors, either.
While no one questions that Mandy Teefey isn’t exactly over the moon about the revival of Jelena, some of the stories that have been floating around about their mother-daughter tensions have been … quite something.
Like rumors that Selena was living with her mother and that her mom had somehow imposed a curfew upon her 25-year-old millionaire celebrity daughter in order to interfere with her romance.
Or rumors that she’s ""demanded"" that Selena get a breathalyzer test while dating Bieber, which … doesn’t make any sense. (Selena’s no alcoholic)
Speaking to Gossip Cop , Mandy Teefey wants to clear things up. First, she talks about Selena’s battle with Lupus.
""When you are young, you feel invincible and she had to learn that she was not able to be your typical 20 year old.""
That is so sad. But, of course, Mandy is so glad that Selena received a life-saving kidney transplant .
""Beyond blessed that we had Francia in our life,"" she said.
Francia Raisa made a truly powerful, selfless choice. And a dangerous one. Having a kidney removed is no joke.
Mandy says that, during the surgery, she was a ""wreck inside not only for my daughter, but for Francia and her family.""
Of course. That had to be a weird feeling. And, understandably, Mandy was very, very emotional throughout the ordeal.
""I am pretty sure I am banned from that hospital [now].""
That’s a lot of nice stuff about Francia, but … how does Mandy describe Selena?
Absolutely. To share her struggle with Lupus with the world, to share the news of her kidney transplant surgery, is a big deal.
But living her life every day, to the fullest, the way that Selana does is truly an act of courage.
Remember, folks — that kidney transplant wasn’t a cure. Lupus has treatments, but no cure. And Selena’s case of Lupus seems to be especially severe.
So, now that she’s being directly interviewed, how does Mandy feel about Selena’s latest reunion with the Biebs?
That much, we knew, but it sure would have been interesting if Mandy were pretending that it’s totally fine.
""Selena can live her life however she wants as long as she is happy, safe, and healthy.""
But the relationship that she describes having with Selena isn’t exactly the feud that we’ve been hearing about.
""Every mother and daughter has disagreements.""
And Mandy denies that her relationship with her daughter began to crumble in 2014 when she stopped being Selena’s manager.
We’d heard reports that Selena’s reunion with Bieber was just one source of conflict .
""We never expected to manage her her whole life. We lost family time because the only time we saw her, we talked business.""
We were going to say that sounds like no fun , but Mandy beat us to it.
""It was no longer fun for any of us, like it was when she was younger and we had to be with her because she was a minor.""
Mandy sounds determined to quash the feud rumors, saying of Selena:
""She is 25 years old and knows what is at stake with her health. I do not control her the way it has been portrayed.""
Well, surely no one really believed that Mandy was somehow giving her adult millionaire celebrity daughter a curfew.
""Selena is an adult and can make her own choices.""
