gossipcop-904884,https://www.self.com/gallery/celebrities-wearing-black-golden-globes,"Golden Globes 2018: Black Dresses, Time’s Up, and Fashion as Activism",950497135266615296	950498946459189248	950500549815873538	950501666951868416	950502087372099584	950502387776712704	950516505279545344	950522740917833730	950529390412271617	950529672818970626	950531595152363525	950532243847614464	950541726191669248	950541743350583297	950557511945515010	950559345804267521	950659495449022466	950674668452630529	952228768319500288,0,self.com,69,"The Golden Globes marks the start of the 2018 awards season. But this year, many female celebrities are eschewing the typical brightly colored dresses and statement-making patterns on the red carpet for something more powerful—all black.
The call to wear black comes from the ambitious Time’s Up initiative, created by 300 actresses and female agents, writers, directors, and executives, including such power players as Eva Longoria , Shonda Rimes , and Reese Witherspoon . The goal of Time’s Up is to combat inequality, sexual harassment, and sexual assault in the workplace; the movement already has a $14 million legal defense fund. Time’s Up is a direct response to the #MeToo movement that has upended industries from politics to Hollywood to restaurants to journalism by exposing sexual harassment and sexual assault in the workplace—and actually, miraculously, holding abusers accountable.
The all-black fashion statement could be classified as a silent protest, but the purpose is loud and clear: It’s a powerful show of support for the many people who are speaking out against their powerful abusers. Longoria told The New York Times , “This is a moment of solidarity, not a fashion moment. For years, we’ve sold these awards shows as women, with our gowns and colors and our beautiful faces and our glamour. This time the industry can’t expect us to go up and twirl around. That’s not what this moment is about.”
See below for a growing list of the actresses who participated in the red carpet blackout at the Golden Globes.
In addition to wearing black, many actresses brought special guests to the Golden Globes red carpet. Meryl Streep arrived alongside activist Ai-jen Poo, director of the National Domestic Workers Alliance and codirector of the Caring Across Generations Campaign .
Debra Messing wore a sparkly black top with a long train over a pair of wide-leg pants to show solidarity with Time's Up. She also took a moment on the red carpet to show support for TV anchor Catt Sadler. “I was so shocked to hear that E! doesn’t believe in paying their female cohost the same as their male cohost,” Messing told E! News on the red carpet. “I miss Catt Sadler, so we stand with her.”
Both Salma Hayek and Ashley Judd were pivotal whistleblowers in the Harvey Weinstein case, and they continue to be a part of the movement by wearing black on the Golden Globes red carpet.
Before hitting the red carpet, Tracee Ellis Ross posted a behind-the-scenes photo on Instagram wearing her Time's Up T-shirt. At the Golden Globes, she wore a black gown with a coordinating headwrap.
Amy Poehler walked the Golden Globes red carpet with Saru Jayaraman, president of Restaurant Opportunities Centers United and director of the Food Labor Research Center .
Catherine Zeta-Jones was stunning in a sheer, long-sleeve gown that grazed the carpet.
Chrissy Metz wore a velvet dress with a sequin bow around the off-the-shoulder neckline.
Mandy Moore adhered to the all-black dress code to support Time's Up, but she also added a splash of red with a belt.
Reese Witherspoon and Eva Longoria were two crucial founders of the Time's Up initiative, and they arrived on the red carpet together in stunning black gowns with extra long trains following behind.
Outlander star Caitriona Balfe wore a gorgeous black gown with floral details at the knees and around her arms.
Actress Emma Watson was joined on the red carpet by Marai Larasi, executive director of Imkaan , a UK organization that works to stop violence against black and minority women.
Dakota Johnson arrived at the Golden Globes in a elegant black ensemble with a bit of bling at the waistline.
Susan Kelechi Watson from This Is Us wore a black wide-leg pantsuit with a navel-grazing neckline.
Get Out actress Allison Williams wore a black gown with a bright orange and silver splash around the bust.",ok
gossipcop-918290,https://www.vanityfair.com/hollywood/2018/03/oscars-2018-jordan-peele-best-original-screenplay-get-out,Oscars 2018: Jordan Peele Wins an Oscar and Makes History,970513185471156224	970513591660171265	970513806765035520	970514310412800000	970514582547517440	970515279145992192	970516219546750978	970516263091847168	970519998283345920	970523905675862017	970524229174149120	970539479357272065	970553179552276480	970555527565594624	970655121817440256,0,vanityfair.com,57,"It only took one film for Jordan Peele to become an Oscar winner. On Sunday night, the writer-director picked up the best-original-screenplay statuette for Get Out, the social thriller that dominated the awards cycle for its acute commentary on race in America. He is the first black screenwriter to win an award in this category.
“This means so much to me,” Peele said. “I stopped writing this movie about 20 times because I thought it was impossible.”
He continued, saying that he eventually persevered because he knew that if it did get made, “people would hear it and people would see it.”
“I want to dedicate this to all the people who raised my voice,” he said, thanking fans, Universal, and Blumhouse. He also thanked his wife, the comedian Chelsea Peretti, “who supported me through this whole process,” and his mother, “who taught me to love, even in the face of hate.”
Once backstage, he tweeted something a little less formal:
Peele’s Get Out was up against The Big Sick, Lady Bird, The Shape of Water, and Three Billboards Outside Ebbing, Missouri. Get Out, thanks to its pop-culture appeal and astounding social relevance, was an early front-runner, and was nominated for four awards overall: best picture, best director (for Peele), best original screenplay (Peele), and best actor ( Daniel Kaluuya ).
Back when it was released last February, the film quickly picked up steam and punctured the zeitgeist, morphing from a well-received low-budget horror hit to a serious awards-season contender. Peele has spent the last few months stumping for the film and picking up barrels of awards from various critics circles and the Directors Guild of America. The social thriller, about a black man who visits his white girlfriend’s family and discovers that things are not all they seem, resonated with audiences and made about $255 million worldwide.
Stay up-to-date with all things Oscars with this year’s live blog .",ok
