gossipcop-920144,https://www.hollywoodreporter.com/live-feed/crazy-girlfriend-end-season-4-rachel-bloom-says-1099019,"'Crazy Ex-Girlfriend' to End With Season 4, Rachel Bloom Says",980896006001840128	980897282286608384	980897459198222337	980897520434974721	980897524969103362	980898212012806144	980898568927109120	980899145949917185	980907043988164611	981146411357138944	981150350920048640	981170866066219008	981616552272367617,0,hollywoodreporter.com,61,"The end of the road for The CW ‘s Crazy Ex-Girlfriend is in sight.
On Monday, the younger-skewing broadcast network announced that Crazy will return for a fourth run in the 2018-19 season. And, according to its star and co-creator, Rachel Bloom , that fourth run will be its final season.
“Just turned on my Wifi on our flight to Chicago and found out that #CrazyExGirlfriend has been renewed for a final season,” Bloom tweeted Monday.
The CW declined comment on if season four will indeed be the show’s endgame. A formal announcement on that is expected to come closer to May’s upfronts, when the broadcast networks pitch their schedules to Madison Avenue ad buyers.
Still, that Crazy would end with its fourth season should come as no surprise. Following the season one finale, Bloom told The Hollywood Reporter that her “ideal number” of seasons is “around four.”
“We’re a show about a very specific journey in a person’s life. And it’s not really a sitcom meant to spit out copies of itself,” Bloom said. “Very early on, we called it a 50-hour movie. I think we’re beyond that now. There’s always going to be plot movement, so there’s only so far I think you can go with that. It could be five, but I think around four would be ideal for us creatively.”
With its third season ending in February, Crazy has aired a total of 44 episodes. (Season one was 18 episodes, while two and three were 13 each.) Still, an abbreviated run could be considered likely for the newly announced fourth season.
What Crazy lacked in overall viewers, the series, which was originally developed as a half-hour comedy for CW corporate sibling Showtime, made up for with critical and awards season love. One of the lowest-rated series on broadcast, Crazy has been an Emmy player for its original music and choreography, and Bloom won a Golden Globe for her starring role, among other accolades.
Next season could see also The CW wrap Jane the Virgin after star Gina Rodriguez recently said the series would return for a “ final season .” A formal announcement on that could also come in May.
Next season will see The CW expand to a sixth night of originals (on Sundays). All told, the network renewed 10 shows Monday and has a record nine pilots as well as a spinoff of The Originals in contention for 2018-19. Still to be determined are the fates of The 100 and iZombie, with rookies Valor and Life Sentence both considered unlikely to return.
Keep track of all the latest renewals and cancellations with THR ‘s handy scorecard and of all the pilot pickups with our handy guide .
Sign up for THR news straight to your inbox every day",ok
gossipcop-883342,https://www.teenvogue.com/gallery/selena-gomez-artist-music-evolution,"Selena Gomez's Artist Evolution, From Disney Channel Dream to 'Revival' Ruler",913722628325638146	913723336026476549	913725931465867265	913726836802285568	913726925570494464	913727600018677761	913727672924196864	913727710899445760	913727901165551616	913729322128740353	913729833430958086	913729836866183168	913729850892013569	913730982322348032	913731426549473280	913731543507587072	913731568283262982	913732415297900545	913735012712878080	913735917717872640	913736058931748865	913736471865167878	913741700023103488	913757269250191360	913775390501437440	913780204916690944	913780401579192325	913780419950268417	913780420902342656	913780425847459843	913780667292508160	913780735840079873	913780763685990400	913780784246460416	913780788822515713	913780860838703105	913780873044140032	913780886793056257	913780950269558784	913780999447891968	913781101335900160	913781174505541632	913781172282544128	913781229094305792	913781267405185024	913781310874882048	913781333993906177	913781332672700416	913781400410755079	913781500591648769	913781546594766854	913781705013678081	913795517800427524	913852145048236032	913860192772616192	913860214406840320	914560549161193480	914577657953685504	914584706452217857	914584956575391744	914588243601117185	914590746375868417	914590870388854789	914594784605622272	914600560975863810	914600826911494145	914603350007656448	914603376821796864	914615167350124544,0,teenvogue.com,77,"Selena Gomez has come a long way with her music. Just when you think you have her all figured out, the Disney Channel alum does a total 180 and switches things up on her fans. So to celebrate our brand-new artist evolution column, we figured taking a look back at all her music would be the perfect way to kick things off.
From releasing the ultimate pop jam ""One and the Same"" to accompany one of her first Disney Channel Original Movies in 2009 with Demi Lovato to doing the ultimate Revival for her 2015 album and working with A$AP Rocky , there’s no type of music Selena isn’t afraid to explore. Most recently she dabbled with the likes of EDM as she released her newest single “It Ain’t Me” with DJ Kygo , who also worked with her collaborator Charlie Puth . Now as she teased new music from the studio over the weekend on Instagram , we can’t help but wonder what she’ll surprise us with next.
The star certainly has her way of keeping people guessing. As you wait for her to wow you with her next hit, get lost in our roundup of all her tracks. (We’ll even take you all the way to her humble beginnings on Barney and Friends . Yes, the one and only purple dinosaur.) Selena has more than proved that she always has something completely different in store for her followers, which may be the very reason why they’re so devoted to her. Click through and let the nostalgia hit you hard.
Before she started recording her own music, Selena got major practice singing the classic “I Love You” song as Gianna on Barney . At just 10 years old, this is also where her friendship with Demi started. The girls met auditioning for the show, so perhaps Disney has the purple dinosaur to thank for discovering two of its biggest stars.
Fast forward to five years later, Selena honed in her voice as the Disney classic’s memorable character, Alex Russo. Wizardry aside, there’s no middle child quite like her. The show essentially launched her career as it ended up being where she debuted her first solo single — which was also the series’ theme song — “Everything Is Not What It Seems.”
All the way down to their adorable music video for the theme song of their joint DCOM Princess Protection Program , Selena and Demi quite literally had a picture-perfect friendship. Just like her role in the movie, “One and the Same” was the moment where Selena narrowed in, believe it or not, her pop punk princess image. She and Demi never strayed away from their pop melodies, but you can really hear the electric guitar riffs that made the song so special.
The actress took music more seriously as she made a bigger effort to cross over with the band she formed under her label Hollywood Records, which she called Selena & the Scene. They released their debut studio album Kiss & Tell in 2009, right in the middle of her Disney series ‘Wizards of Waverly Place,’ so of course, Disney had her back in promoting their singles. (Remember when watching music videos on TV were still a thing?!) Around the same time the album came out, Selena drew pop punk inspirations from her fellow Texan band Forever the Sickest Kids as she appeared in a remix of their hit single “Whoa Oh! (Me vs. Everyone).” She gave a solid Paramore performance that would have made their lead singer Hayley Williams proud. It all came full circle as Selena balanced out these heavy punk influences with her signature pop sound with the album’s hits including “Falling Down” and “Naturally.”
She had Katy Perry to thank for her second studio album. The “Teenage Dream” singer pushed Selena to real in even more of her pop sound with the Selenator anthem “A Year Without Rain.” This was really the moment went Selena departed from the pop punk phase she had as a teenager. For those of you who used to have a ball at Hot Topic and rock knee high black and white checked socks, don’t be ashamed if your heart broke a little bit here.
We have to admit, this is really when Selena went full-on pop. Her third album featuring hits like “Who Says,"" “Love You Like a Love Song,” and “Whiplash” — which was actually written by none other than pop legend Britney Spears . There’s no doubt pulling off a Britney original got her to make the switch. Her biggest hit “Who Says” from the album was also one of the few times Selena paid tribute to her heritage and released a full Spanish version of a song. If you haven’t listed to it, look up “Dices” and get ready for your mind to be blown.
Selena took a hiatus from the Scene and officially went solo when she released her fourth studio album Stars Dance , just one year after the finale of ‘Wizards of Waverly Place.’ This time around, she went a different route with her pop music. The star experimented with bold electronic beats, creating music her fans can really dance to. Play the album’s hits “Come and Get It” and “Slow Down” and you’ll know exactly what we mean. Interestingly enough, she cited Justin Bieber ’s biggest collaborators Skrillex as inspiration for this album, but contrary to popular belief her step in this direction apparently had nothing to do with him.
Leading up to the release of Revival , her biggest album yet, Selena jumped on the EDM bandwagon and collaborated with ex Zedd for their hit collab “I Want You to Know.” This album elevated her mainstream pop sound and resulted a Selena we’ve never heard before, especially with her track “Good for You” featuring rapper A$AP Rocky. Beyond delivering unexpected music that topped the charts, Selena gave a candid account of her life through this body of work. As this was after her heavily publicized breakup with Justin, she became vulnerable and let fans into her life in the spotlight. With other hits including “Same Old Love,” “Hands to Myself,” and “Kill ‘Em With Kindness,” it was a Selena no one really saw coming.
Charlie Puth and Kygo were her latest collaborators proving how everything in her career contributed to her hits today. She and Charlie’s “We Don’t Talk Anymore” showcase the pop breakup ballads she’s known for, but her pop EDM collab with Kygo more than proves she’s not done growing. Selena is the type of artist that thrives in challenging herself and we guarantee “It Ain’t Me” is just the beginning. We can hardly wait for what she drops next!
Like us on Facebook to stay in the know.",ok
