over Hollywood.
“I don’t want to profit from my work on the film,” Chalamet said, after first saying that due to “contractual obligations,” he can’t speak directly about his decision to work with Allen. That said, Chalamet indicated that he initially joined the Allen project because he is “a young actor trying to walk in the footsteps of more seasoned actors I admire.”
“But I am learning that a good role isn’t the only criteria for accepting a job—that has become much clearer to me in the past few months, having witnessed the birth of a powerful movement intent on ending injustice, inequality and, above all, silence,” he continued.
Chalamet ultimately decided to donate his salary to various charities because he wants “to be worthy of standing shoulder to shoulder with the brave artists who are fighting for all people to be treated with the respect and dignity they deserve.”
His high-profile co-stars in the film, including Selena Gomez, Jude Law, Liev Schreiber, and Elle Fanning, have largely remained silent on this matter—even when asked about it directly . Chalamet’s co-star Rebecca Hall, however—who also starred in Allen’s 2008 film Vicky Cristina Barcelona —has said she regrets working on the project and has donated her salary from the film to Time’s Up.
Chalamet has been asked several times in the last few months about his decision to work with Allen—a controversial figure most recently because of accusations from his estranged daughter. Dylan Farrow, who was adopted by Allen and Mia Farrow, has alleged that Allen sexually abused her when she was a child. Allen has denied the claims.
Farrow made headlines again in December with an op-ed for the Los Angeles Times that asked why the #MeToo movement—which has swept up the careers of alleged predators like Harvey Weinstein —had not yet had an impact on Allen’s career. Farrow specifically called out stars who have worked with Allen recently, including Greta Gerwig and Blake Lively, who danced around acknowledging the allegations against Allen in various interviews. “It breaks my heart when women and men I admire work with Allen, then refuse to answer questions about it,” Farrow wrote.
But in the weeks since her op-ed was published, there has been a sea change in the industry response to Allen. Gerwig, for example, eventually told The New York Times that she regrets working with Allen and “will not work for him again.” She was largely inspired to take that stance after reading pieces by Dylan Farrow.
Just a few days later, Mira Sorvino, who won an Oscar after starring in Allen’s 1995 film Mighty Aphrodite, penned an open letter in which she apologized directly to Farrow. “I never personally experienced what has now been described as inappropriate behavior toward young girls,” she said. “But this does not excuse my turning a blind eye to your story simply because I wanted desperately for it not to be so.”
Last November, Ellen Page wrote a lengthy Facebook post as well, declaring that making an Allen film is “the biggest regret of my career.” She starred in his 2012 film To Rome with Love.
And just a month before that, actor Griffin Newman, who has a small role in A Rainy Day in New York, announced that he was donating his salary to RAINN. He explained his decision in a Twitter thread, saying that he believes Allen is “guilty” and “deeply” regrets his decision to work for the director. Newman made his decision public just a few days after The New York Times ran its first explosive report about Weinstein.
Allen has also been denounced by several high-profile stars who are currently championing Time’s Up. In a recent interview with Oprah Winfrey about the movement and the Time’s Up Legal Defense Fund, actress Natalie Portman said she believes Dylan, while Shonda Rhimes said she hope’s Allen’s time is truly up.
