‘The sweetest thing I can remember is when she moved into Buckingham Palace,’ says Virginia. ‘She liked to invite us round there sometimes. We were there when her wedding veil arrived. I think it was Carolyn and me and Diana and we were taking it in turns trying it on and imagining what was coming. It was surreal, wandering around her bedroom in the palace wearing the veil.’
The giggling girls demanded of Diana, ‘How are you going to curtsy?... Are you going to go low?... How low can you go?’ Diana had to curtsy to the Queen at the top of the aisle in St Paul’s. On the day her curtsy was textbook. Textbook also was Prince Charles’s gesture to the girls in the flat. ‘He sent us a huge bouquet of flowers to thank us for our support,’ says Virginia. ‘That was really sweet.’
o one encapsulated the wedding euphoria more than Molimea ‘Ilolahia. He was a waiter for the King of Tonga and had met the Queen on her visits.
Hearing of the royal wedding, he wrote to her private secretary Sir William Heseltine explaining that his greatest ambition was to serve at Buckingham Palace, if only for a day, and could he fix it. Sir William did, with the Queen herself.
So on the eve of the wedding, when the Queen held a reception at the palace, Moli was there on duty, with a tray of drinks to hand out. ‘There were three reigning sovereigns present, as well as maharajahs, several de-throned monarchs and all of the Commonwealth governors-general,’ chuckles Sir William. ‘One of the VIPs was the president of Gambia [President Dawda Jawara] who was deposed while he was at the party.
‘But when the Queen came in, the first person her eye lit on was Moli and he was the first person she walked across the room to greet. All the assembled VIPs thought he must be some even higher VIP. But he was just a waiter.’
