LuAnn de Lesseps became nationally famous as an original cast member of Bravo's ""The Real Housewives of New York City,"" appearing across the show's first 13 seasons and transforming her ""Countess"" persona into a career that extended well beyond reality television. Before ""RHONY,"" LuAnn worked as a licensed practical nurse and professional model and spent years living internationally after marrying French entrepreneur and aristocrat Count Alexandre de Lesseps . Her later ventures have included the book ""Class with the Countess,"" dance singles including ""Money Can't Buy You Class,"" and an unexpectedly durable live cabaret career that began with ""#CountessAndFriends"" in 2018 and eventually expanded into a multi-year national touring deal with Live Nation. She subsequently starred alongside Sonja Morgan in ""Luann & Sonja: Welcome to Crappie Lake,"" appeared on ""The Real Housewives Ultimate Girls Trip: RHONY Legacy,"" participated in the dating series ""Bravo's Love Hotel,"" and joined the core cast of 2026's ""The Real Housewives Ultimate Girls Trip: Roaring 20th.""
LuAnn Nadeau was born on May 17, 1965, in Berlin, Connecticut. She grew up in a large family with six siblings. Her father operated a construction business, and several members of the Nadeau family later remained involved with the company.
Before entering entertainment, LuAnn trained as a licensed practical nurse. She worked in a nursing home caring for elderly patients and has said the experience helped develop the interpersonal skills she would later use throughout her television and performing career. LuAnn subsequently entered modeling and signed with Wilhelmina Models, with modeling work taking her outside the United States and introducing her to an international social world very different from her upbringing in Connecticut.
In 1993, LuAnn married Count Alexandre de Lesseps, a French businessman descended from the family of Ferdinand de Lesseps, the diplomat and developer associated with the Suez Canal.
Through the marriage, LuAnn became Countess de Lesseps, a title that would later become central to her public persona on ""The Real Housewives of New York City."" The couple had two children, Victoria and Noel, and lived internationally during their marriage.
LuAnn and Alexandre separated after approximately 16 years together and divorced in 2009. Although the marriage ended, LuAnn continued using the Countess title socially and professionally until her second marriage.
LuAnn joined the original cast of ""The Real Housewives of New York City"" when the series premiered on Bravo in March 2008. The original ensemble also included Bethenny Frankel and Ramona Singer .
LuAnn initially stood out for her aristocratic title, emphasis on etiquette, and formal approach to social situations. Over time, however, her willingness to laugh at herself, discuss her romantic life openly, and participate in the show's increasingly chaotic group dynamics made her one of the franchise's longest-running personalities.
She appeared as a full-time cast member during seasons one through five. LuAnn was credited as a ""Friend of the Housewives"" during season six, when she was spending less time in New York, before returning to the main cast for seasons seven through 13.
Her tenure included friendships and conflicts with many of the show's best-known cast members, including Sonja Morgan and Dorinda Medley . Several of LuAnn's lines and storylines became recurring pieces of Bravo culture, including ""Be cool, don't be all uncool,"" her insistence that ""money can't buy you class,"" and the transformation of her Countess identity from a source of social status into a self-aware entertainment brand.
Bravo replaced the longtime ""RHONY"" cast with an entirely new ensemble beginning with season 14, ending LuAnn's regular run on the original series after 13 seasons.
In 2009, LuAnn published ""Class with the Countess: How to Live with Elegance and Flair."" The book combined autobiographical material with advice on manners, confidence, entertaining, and social etiquette.
The following year, she released the dance-pop single ""Money Can't Buy You Class."" The song's title was drawn directly from her reality-TV persona and became one of the most recognizable musical releases associated with the ""Real Housewives"" franchise.
LuAnn followed it with ""Chic, C'est la Vie"" in 2011 and ""Girl Code"" in 2015. She later released ""Feelin' Jovani"" in 2019, a song inspired by one of her most memorable ""RHONY"" storylines, followed by additional tracks including ""F-Bombs on the G with the OGs,"" ""What Do I Want for Christmas?"" and ""Marry F Kill.""
Rather than functioning primarily as a conventional recording artist, LuAnn increasingly used her music as part of a broader live entertainment act combining singing, comedy, storytelling, audience interaction, and references to her years on reality television.
LuAnn launched ""#CountessAndFriends"" at Feinstein's/54 Below in New York City in February 2018. What initially appeared to be a novelty extension of her television fame quickly developed into a genuine second career.
The show combined musical performances with comedy and stories about LuAnn's personal life and ""RHONY"" experiences. Early performances featured celebrity guests including Rachel Dratch and Sonja Morgan, and strong ticket demand led LuAnn to take the show on the road.
Live Nation ultimately signed ""#CountessAndFriends"" to a multi-year national touring deal. The production sold out venues around the country and established live performance as a meaningful business for LuAnn rather than merely a promotional extension of ""RHONY.""
Her later productions became increasingly ambitious, with setlists combining her own songs, including ""Money Can't Buy You Class,"" ""Chic C'est la Vie,"" and ""Feelin' Jovani,"" with covers of pop, rock, disco, and Broadway standards.
By the mid-2020s, LuAnn was regularly playing established theaters and music venues across the United States and internationally. Her touring continued through 2026 with ""The Love Tour,"" including North American dates followed by performances in the United Kingdom.
In 2023, LuAnn and Sonja Morgan received their own Bravo spinoff, ""Luann & Sonja: Welcome to Crappie Lake.""
The eight-episode series sent the longtime Manhattan socialites to Benton, Illinois, where LuAnn and Sonja worked with residents on local improvement projects, including building a playground, helping an animal shelter, renovating a motel, and staging a community variety show.
The series deliberately played on the contrast between their glamorous New York personas and life in a small Midwestern town. Rather than relying entirely on the conflicts that had driven ""RHONY,"" ""Crappie Lake"" leaned more heavily into comedy and the friendship between LuAnn and Sonja.
LuAnn returned to the broader Housewives franchise later in 2023 as a cast member of ""The Real Housewives Ultimate Girls Trip: RHONY Legacy.""
The Peacock series reunited LuAnn with Sonja Morgan, Ramona Singer, Dorinda Medley, Kelly Killoren Bensimon, and Kristen Taekman for a trip to St. Barts. The location deliberately recreated one of the best-known vacations from the original ""RHONY.""
In 2026, Bravo again selected LuAnn as one of seven central cast members for ""The Real Housewives Ultimate Girls Trip: Roaring 20th,"" a commemorative series celebrating 20 years of the ""Real Housewives"" franchise.
In 2025, LuAnn joined Shannon Beador , Gizelle Bryant , and Ashley Darby on the Bravo dating series ""Bravo's Love Hotel.""
The show brought the four single Housewives to a luxury hotel in Los Cabos, Mexico, where they met and dated a group of eligible men. Joel Kim Booster served as the host and ""Love Concierge.""
LuAnn ultimately left the show in a relationship with contestant James Quine, extending her personal-life storyline into another Bravo franchise while introducing her to an audience beyond the original ""RHONY.""
LuAnn has also taken occasional scripted acting roles. In 2011, she guest-starred on ""Law & Order: Special Victims Unit"" in the episode ""Bully,"" playing a wealthy socialite.
She later appeared in the film ""Maternal Secrets,"" which was filmed in Bermuda and was also released under the title ""Secrets of the Sisterhood."" Acting has remained a comparatively small part of her career compared with reality television, music, and cabaret.
On December 24, 2017, LuAnn was arrested in Palm Beach, Florida, following an incident at a hotel. Initial charges included disorderly intoxication, resisting arrest, battery on a law-enforcement officer, and making threats against a public servant. The arrest occurred only months after the end of her second marriage, and LuAnn subsequently entered an alcohol-treatment program.
In July 2018, she reached a plea agreement. LuAnn pleaded guilty to misdemeanor charges including battery, disorderly intoxication, and trespassing, while the felony charge of resisting an officer with violence was dropped. Her sentence included one year of probation, 50 hours of community service, attendance at Alcoholics Anonymous meetings, restrictions on alcohol consumption, and other conditions.
LuAnn entered treatment for a second time in July 2018, causing her to miss filming of the ""RHONY"" season 10 reunion. Andy Cohen and the show's cast subsequently discussed her absence during the reunion.
In May 2019, LuAnn was briefly taken into custody after allegations that she had violated the terms of her probation. The court ultimately allowed her to continue under revised conditions. She successfully completed probation on August 28, 2019, and the Palm Beach County case was closed.
LuAnn married Count Alexandre de Lesseps in 1993, and they welcomed two children, Victoria and Noel. The couple divorced in 2009.
On December 31, 2016, LuAnn married businessman Tom D'Agostino Jr. in Palm Beach, Florida. The marriage was heavily featured on ""RHONY,"" including disputes among cast members over D'Agostino's romantic history and behavior before the wedding.
LuAnn announced in August 2017 that the marriage was ending after approximately seven months. Their divorce was finalized later that year.
During her marriage to Alexandre, LuAnn owned a substantial home in Bridgehampton, New York. The couple reportedly acquired the land for approximately $500,000 , spent around $1.4 million building the residence, and later invested approximately $2 million in renovations.
The Bridgehampton property was eventually sold for a reported $8 million . The disposition of those proceeds later became part of a family dispute. Alexandre and the couple's two children alleged in a 2018 lawsuit that provisions of the divorce agreement required LuAnn to create a trust for Victoria and Noel and place half of her ownership interest in a replacement Hamptons home into that trust. The lawsuit was eventually discontinued.
In 2013, LuAnn paid $3.1 million for a waterfront home in Sag Harbor, New York. The approximately 2,500-square-foot historic residence sits on Sag Harbor Cove and includes four bedrooms, multiple fireplaces, water views, and a private dock.
She listed the home for approximately $6.25 million in 2018 while considering a move farther upstate but ultimately withdrew it from the market. LuAnn subsequently began offering the Sag Harbor property as an expensive summer rental, at times asking roughly $100,000 or more for individual peak-season months.
Public property records continue to list LuAnn as the owner of the Sag Harbor home. The Village of Sag Harbor's 2026 assessment roll placed its estimated market value at approximately $6.1 million .
In 2018, LuAnn also paid approximately $1 million for a distinctive midcentury round house in Port Ewen, New York, overlooking the Hudson River. She listed that property for $1.15 million in 2020 and sold it later that year for approximately $1.2 million .",ok
