When will the Queen take the train to Sandringham at Christmas?
You agree you are 18 or over. We use your sign-up to provide content in ways you've consented to and to improve our understanding of you. This may include adverts from us and third parties based on our knowledge of you. You can unsubscribe at any time. More Info .
An official date has not been released for when the Queen will catch the train to her Christmas hideout this year.
But she is known to travel on a regular commuter train to and from Norfolk every year.
The Queen typically arrives in King’s Lynn station, after travelling from London King’s Cross at around 10.45am, along with her royal entourage.
A ticket is believed to cost around £56.10, as she reportedly travels in first class on the Thameslink service.
Her Majesty has been taking the 90-minute train London to Norfolk and then back again for years, so she is a regular commuter.
Back in 2014, a passenger apparently bumped into the Queen’s security with her luggage , according to The Telegraph
Graeme Pratt, the station manager, said to the Eastern Daily Press in 2016: ""It's an honour to have her use the train service.
""I asked her if she enjoyed her stay in Norfolk, to which she said she did.""
The Queen is one of the most well-travelled British monarchs in history.
She has visited over 130 countries during her reign, including Canada and Australia.
Queen Elizabeth and her husband Prince Philip also visitsthe same place every summer for their annual holiday.
