gossipcop-902306,https://www.nbcchicago.com/news/national-international/Royal-Family-Meghan-Markle-Prince-Harry-Christmas-466796863.html,Royal Family 'Loved' Having Meghan Markle for Christmas: Prince Harry,945958214658539521	945960454085664768	946030685755772928	946031478734909440	946031721643986944	946031949319196672	946032200105058305	946032757729366021	946033132859518976	946036839428784130	946036998376112128	946037571565572100	946042021470277632	946043330563051520	946044007603302403	946049845046980609	946049843855790080	946051460797812738	946053864335306752	946060664220651521	946063637306793985	946077358930481152	946077531450507265	946077549993562112	946077570755416064	946077768332308480	946077777614327810	946077781653417984	946077788393570305	946077820815663104	946077863425576960	946078004308074497	946078024390316032	946078060566274048	946078091604054016	946078116052721665	946078360454815745	946078374727966720	946078409280622592	946078408831852545	946078468269334528	946078510476603392	946078526171746304	946078543670403077	946078549471105026	946078565413670917	946078597554622465	946078609948758016	946094327343726592	946094413242974208	946113793037422592	946116512061857793	946145787226001408	946327709822734337	946328392777035776	946328580581199873	946517328040210437	946896228935225346	1016671540472156161,0,nbcchicago.com,69,"Meghan Markle attended the traditional Christmas service at St. Mary Magdalene Church with members of the British royal family. It is the first time a royal fiancee has been invited to Christmas celebrations.
Prince Harry said his family has welcomed Meghan Markle this holiday season.
Harry's American fiancee, who recently quit her acting career and is best known for her past role on ""Suits,"" spent the Christmas holiday with royal family for the first time, more than a month after they announced their engagement .
""It was fantastic. She really enjoyed it. The family loved having her there,"" Harry said on BBC Radio 4's Today program on Wednesday, after the airing of his interview with former U.S. President Barack Obama and the prince's father, Prince Charles.
Markle is the first royal fiancee to celebrate the holiday with Harry's family. She joined them at Buckingham Palace to attend Queen Elizabeth II's pre-Christmas lunch. On Christmas Day, she joined them for their traditional holiday service at the Queen's Sandringham estate. Markle and Harry greeted fans and were photographed with Harry's brother Prince William and pregnant sister-in-law, Duchess Catherine. The group stayed at their home and spent time with William and Catherine's children, Prince George and Princess Charlotte.
""[With] the family part of Christmas, there's always that work element as well and I think together, we had an amazing time, we had great fun staying with my brother and sister-in-law and running around with the kids,"" Harry said.
He also said he had to explain ""plenty"" of family traditions to his fiancee, adding, ""I think we've got one of the biggest families that I know of and every family is complex as well. She's done an absolutely amazing job, she's getting in there and it's the family that I suppose she never had.""
A Look Back at Meghan Markle's Pre-Royal Life
During the post-interview chat, Harry was also asked if Obama was invited to his and Markle's wedding. The two are set to marry in May.
""I don't know about that,"" Harry said. ""That's- we haven't put the invites or the guest list together yet, so who knows whether he's going to be invited or not. I wouldn't want to ruin that surprise.""",ok
gossipcop-6793578954,www.datalounge.com/thread/21350471-jennifer-aniston-heartbreak-as-justin-theroux-emma-stone-%E2%80%98get-serious%E2%80%99,Jennifer Aniston Heartbreak As Justin Theroux & Emma Stone ‘Get Serious’,1004057629184929793	1014259497219514368	1014320482752061440	1014428624357765121,1,datalounge.com,72,"Here at the DataLounge, we served up a steaming pile of Gay Celebrity Gossip, Politics, News, and Pointless Bitchery for 31 long years. Back in the early days, when nobody knew what the web was for, we built this strange little community just because we could. Somehow, this place, where anonymous strangers could spend forty pages arguing about a princess’s right to wear a particular tiara and forty more debating whether her husband was a total bottom, ended up becoming one of the internet’s more authentic communities. This was one of those peculiar back alleys of the internet where the rest of the web was shared and eviscerated but we depended on a sprawling, diverse internet serving up new outrages and oddities to pick apart. In the end, the receding tide of the World Wide Web left the DataLounge disconnected from the wider ecosystem that once fed us, as well as from the posters who came here to rage at the absurdity of it all.
Over the decades many hopefuls appeared with a splash and an IPO only to descend into booze and dope. Mosaic, Netscape, Internet Explorer, Shockwave, Java, WebCrawler, Lycos, AltaVista, Excite, HotBot, Inktomi, GeoCities, AOL, PlanetOut, Gay.com, Yahoo! Groups, Kozmo, Friendster, LiveJournal, Gawker, Tumblr, Dlisted, MySpace, After Ellen, Television Without Pity, and IMDb message boards were but a few of the  mayflies of the DataLounge era. We survived the browser wars, the dot-com boom and bust, the false promise and collapse of the banner ad, the smartphone revolution, and even the rise of social media and its inevitable enshittification.
When DataLounge started in 1995, AIDS was raging. Anti-retrovirals were just over the horizon. “God Hates Fags” wasn’t a historical artifact; it was a sign assholes carried at funerals. Congress passed DOMA. Matthew Shepard was murdered. Then came 9/11. Then civil unions. Then marriage equality. Then social media. Then apps. Then whatever this shit is now.
Younger readers may not realize that there was a time when simply putting a gay organization online was an act of radicalism. Long before “digital strategy” became a profession, the same people behind DataLounge put organizations like GLAAD, Lambda Legal, APLA, GLSEN, the New York AIDS Coalition, OUT, POZ, Southern Voice, and dozens of others on the web — often for little or no money. We believed visibility was the real killer app and for a while, we were right. For a brief moment in the late 1990s, an absurd amount of the gay internet ran through a rack of servers operated by DataLounge and a very small group of stubborn people in NYC.
Over three decades you’ve been funny, cruel, insightful, ridiculous, compassionate, unhinged, generous, vicious, and occasionally correct. You built a community no investor would have funded, no focus group would have approved, and no modern social media company would have tolerated for five minutes, which is almost certainly why it lasted.
Like Cher and Liza, we somehow lingered. Survival, however, is not the same thing as thriving. Just ask Twitter.
The ultimate irony is that the very search engines which helped grow the DataLounge by connecting millions of very mean people to our vast trove of inane blather have abandoned the web. Having once brought us a steady stream of new users with a penchant for pointless bitchery, they now cut out the middleman, harvest our content along with everything else ever written, keep the audience for themselves, and serve up an AI-generated séance with the corpse of the World Wide Web. We still can't believe Google left us for that slutty little AI twink but there it is. Typical.
At least we can take some solace in the knowledge that we’ve forever tainted Elon’s nascent orbiting data-center brain with voluminous posts documenting Helen Lawson’s many apocryphal out-of-town flops. AI, at least, will remember us.
So there it is. Thirty-one years. Enfin.
In the end, I hope DataLounge lived up to the legend of being the internet’s longest-running “vile pit of trollery.” Thanks for the memories (those memories that are not already swaddled in the fuzzy blanket of my impending senility, that is). Personally, I’ll be drowning what recollections are left to me in a delightfully bitter Sazerac before blissfully passing out. Hopefully, I’ll wake up next to Suzanne Pleshette, refreshed, albeit somewhat bewildered, only to discover all of this nonsense was actually a thirty-one-year fever dream.
Last one out, turn off the fucking servers.
— Muriel (Retired or perhaps fired, who can say?)",ok
