Ms. Dykstra has presented her side (I read her piece in its entirety).   If it helps her then good for her.  What is so disconcerting to me is that her feelings have resulted in real life consequences for Mr. Hardwick.
A careful reading of her piece shows that no crimes were committed.  It appears that Mr. Hardwick asked her to do certain things and that she, for her own reasons, agreed and complied.  She appears to believe, now, that the things he asked of her were uncalled for and abusive.  Fine.  But she agreed to do them- that is on her.
All of us, men and women, make sacrifices in relationships.  Nothing is ever completely ‘even.’  Sometimes are sacrifices are not reciprocated in kind or by degree.
Ms. Dykstra applied her interpretation of events as the motivation of another person.  I have a real problem with that.  I also have a problem with people saying that Mr. Hardwick is a abuser.  We do not have context or evidence of that.  We have feelings- presented from one person in a relationship.
If you, man or woman, are pressured to have sex and do not want to have sex say ‘no.’  Just say it.  If you go ahead and consent anyway then you have done just that- consented.  People cannot read minds.
We are all free to make decisions in our life.  When we turn 18 we are given the freedom to make those choices because we are adults.  Ms. Dykstra is an adult.  She must own her decisions.  She can regret them but her regrets do not mean Mr. Hardwick was an abuser.  She chose to distance herself from family and friends.  She chose to make her relationship more important than other things.
And she is choosing, now, to try to destroy a persons career.  I hope Mr. Hardwick sues her and she is forced to present her ‘evidence.’",ok
