gossipcop-845895,https://www.dailymail.co.uk/tvshowbiz/article-2593045/Im-excited-ageing-process-says-Emma-age-23-Actress-says-interested-women-arent-perfect.html,Emma Watson admits 'I don't date people who are famous',,0,dailymail.co.uk,55,"Published: 20:44 EDT, 30 March 2014 | Updated: 09:50 EDT, 31 March 2014
Unlike many actresses her age, Emma Watson has always steered clear of having a relationship with a fellow celebrity.
But the 23-year-old actress has now opened up about her love life decisions, insisting that while she prefers to date civilians, she doesn't like the way her relationship with them propels them into the public eye.
Emma is currently in a relationship with 21-year-old Oxford University student Matt Janney.
'I don't date famous people': Emma Watson is dating Oxford University student Matt Janney, but said she finds it difficult when a 'normal' person is thrust into the spotlight due to a romance with her
She told Australia's Elle magazine: ' I don't date people who are famous, and I don't think it's fair that, all of a sudden, intimate details of their personal life are public as a direct result of me.
Emma, who first shot to fame as Hermione Granger in Harry Potter, also revealed that her child star past sometimes makes it difficult for her to secure more mature roles.
In action: Matt (left) playing rugby for Oxford during a match against Cambridge
Opening up: Emma spoke to Australian Elle magazine about her love life
She said: ' In my downtime I don't sex myself up much. Sometimes I have a hard time convincing directors that I can play more adult roles.'
But in her personal life, Emma is doing her best to get rid of her 'good girl' image, admitting that she recently broke into a hotel swimming pool at 3am.
She said: 'It was at a hotel, it had a gate around it. My friends turned around and basically I was gone.
'The next thing they saw was me 7ft up in the air, scaling the fence. I shouldn't be able to get away with what I'm getting away with.'
Emma also revealed that she will be marking her graduation from Brown University later this year with an alcohol-fuelled celebration.
She said: 'I will be there and I will be throwing a huge party and getting very drunk.'
Emma's interview with Australian Elle magazine comes after she opened up about the ageing process in a chat with the Sunday Times Style magazine.
She said: ' I can take criticism on the chin a lot more. As a younger woman, that pressure got me down but I’ve made my peace with it. With airbrushing and digital manipulation, fashion can project an unobtainable image that’s dangerously unhealthy.
'I’m excited about the ageing process. I’m more interested in women who aren’t perfect. They’re more compelling.’
She said: 'I'm excited about the ageing process. I'm more interested in women who aren't perfect. They're more compelling'
She said sometimes I have a hard time convincing directors that I can play more adult roles
Emma added that rather than ask a celebrity stylist for sartorial advice she turns to her mother or friends: ‘I love showing them images and asking, ‘What do you think of this dress?’ I call on my boyfriends for advice all the time,’ she says, before quickly correcting herself. ‘I mean friends who are boys, and my girlfriends.’
Miss Watson left Harry Potter behind in 2011 and said all that mattered at the time was university: ‘I didn’t even know if I wanted to be an actress.’
She enrolled at Brown in the US and said she enjoyed the anonymity the American press gave her but her fame inevitably came back to haunt her.
Recalling the first day she walked into the canteen, she said: ‘Everyone went completely silent and turned around to look at me.
‘I had to say to myself, ‘it’s ok, you can do this.’ You just have to take a deep breath and gather your courage. I have moments where I will walk into a bar and it will take me making a joke to put people at ease, to realise I am just a girl.’",ok
