gossipcop-900424,https://www.vanityfair.com/hollywood/2018/03/morgan-spurlock-turner-tnt-docuseries-lawsuit,Admitted Harasser Morgan Spurlock Sued After Abandoning TV Series About Women’s Issues,941739156685942785	941744306896846848	941744826722209793	941751228832215040	941751755255242753	941757269619777536	941761597772623872	941768614482497536	941772182388174848	941780283942146048	941789832375873541	941789842782015489	941789841716645889	941790021404815362	941790055290621952	941790119295619073	941790271351779329	941790315496857600	941790318852235266	941790329048584194	941790337047113729	941790348019462144	941790369414549504	941790534137450496	941790535886438401	941790559571558400	941790618325483524	941790620179365888	941790797795680256	941790807136366592	941790834449625088	941790864455688193	941791015379329025	941791028851470336	941791065601921024	941791072333762560	941791140285747200	941957203883421696	942360948391792641,0,vanityfair.com,86,"After addressing a rape allegation and confessing to sexually harassing a former assistant, Morgan Spurlock all but disappeared from Hollywood last December. The Oscar-nominated documentarian’s social-media feeds went quiet and his projects were halted—most conspicuously, an unfortunately timed TNT docuseries titled Who Rules the World?, which was to examine the unique issues facing modern women.
Thanks to Spurlock’s admission of sexual harassment, it seemed best for him to back away, leaving this project to the network. But Spurlock apparently didn’t make a clean enough break from the series. According to Deadline , Turner Entertainment Networks is now suing Spurlock for botched communication with the network, as well as failing to give back funds that were earmarked for the show.
In the suit, which was filed on Tuesday in a federal court in Los Angeles, Turner’s lawyers argue that Spurlock’s contract was breached and that he failed to return funds, The Guardian notes. The sum has not been disclosed. Per Deadline, Turner requested financial data from the show once the company took over production, but did not get a response from Spurlock or his production company, Warrior Poets. In addition, it requested Spurlock and his team return production funds, but again did not receive a response.
“Under information and belief, due to Spurlock’s Twitter confession and the resulting fall-out from the admissions, Warrior Poets is having serious internal problems and may not have the funds to compensate TEN for the breach of contract,” lawyer Neal Levin wrote in the suit, according to The Guardian, asking for an injunction to be placed on Warrior Poets’ account so the production funds would not be “depleted.”
Issues began arising for Spurlock in December after he posted a confessional essay addressing sexual misconduct claims that had been made about him. He wrote about a girl who accused him of rape in college, though there were ultimately no charges or investigations afterward. “She believed she was raped. That’s why I’m part of the problem,” Spurlock wrote.
He also confessed to sexually harassing a former assistant, as well as cheating on all of his past partners. He later wrote on Twitter that he was “seeking help” for his behavior. Shortly afterward, it was announced that Spurlock was stepping down from his position at Warrior Poets. His film Super Size Me 2 was dropped from the Sundance Film Festival lineup, and he lost his executive-producer credit on Showtime’s The Trade. Who Rules the World? was set to be a collaboration with Sarah Jessica Parker’s Pretty Matches and Refinery29, but production was halted in December. Representatives for Spurlock and Warrior Poets have not yet responded to Vanity Fair’s request for comment.",ok
