If you REALLY think, that concentration camps are a subject you can make SNL jokes about, then you are morally depraved beyond human recognition. Or very uneducated. And, no, being a Jew like Larry David, doesn’t make it O.K., it’s only worse. The difference to people like Mel Brooks is, that he made fun of the Nazis, not the suffering of Holocaust victims. Usually you find Larry David’s kind of ‘humor’ only in Neo Nazi circles.
There’s enough anti-semitism in the world that a Jew doesn’t need to make humour about the Shoah on anational stage. While some can argue it’s just humour – relax and stop over reacting, I completely disagree. I find that for someone deemed ‘funny’ he couldn’t have found something else to talk about in his monologue other than was he said.
Larry David’s monologue was DISGUSTING and offensive. He should be ashamed of himself.
This isn’t an issue of being politically correct.
I’m not too impressed with the people who just dismiss the words as humour.
I just love how this is the most Larry David thing that could ever happen. It’s like, straight out of a new Curbed episode.
Well done!  Tragedy + time = humor. Hence, the cliche, “too soon?” Humor is a tool with which you appreciate and endure the good, the bad and the ugly; and the instrument that allows us to laugh at and recognize our own prejudices and failings. It sometimes gives voice to the otherwise “unspeakable” and uncomfortable thoughts we have all had but dared not admit. Some offensive humor isn’t funny, and some is. Concentration camps aren’t funny but when juxtaposed cleverly with the human condition can be.
As another poster said. Larry has never been a great stand up. But he has created two extremely funny TV series. No, the jokes were not funny, but they were just jokes. I’m Jewish and wasn’t the least bit offended. Watch Louis C.K. 2010 Hilarious special on Netflix. He has a very funny sequence about having sex with dead children. Far more offensive and much, much funnier.
Because a guy is Jewish, it’s okay to have material that attempts humor about the Holocaust. I suppose that under the law, it’s fine, but at some point, common sense needs one to ponder what they say. If you want to make a point about Trump and Weinstein, ( and all the others too), then do so using metaphors that don’t insult.",ok
gossipcop-875218,https://www.celebritynetworth.com/richest-celebrities/authors/carole-radziwill-net-worth/,Carole Radziwill Net Worth,900331827298217985	900331887788294145	900332024501854208	900333696405958656	900333802400174085	900333912613769217	900334675880792064	900334707010940929	900334721774878721	900334769329852416	900334781883445249	900334813227429888	900334864943251456	900334863584296960	900334861776547840	900336945913581569	900338114652065792	900338909791563776	900342022698958848	900342880425971712	900348178918256640	900353388784754689	900361665547386881	900365558880780288	900366686192140288	900367590345891840	900367604040306689	900367613242593280	900367635468009476	900367822206910464	900367832084623360	900367831715401728	900367859821428736	900367886836826112	900368132694519808	900368248205524992	900368252685123584	900368372759748609	900368518410944512	900368610543116289	900368653555781632	900368676712579073	900368799861542912	900369112215437313	900369186521743361	900369208738971648	900369315442155520	900369348115783682	900369395725123584	900369424053489664	900369674768179200	900369709559726081	901568315730141185,0,celebritynetworth.com,26,"Carole Radziwill is an American journalist, author, producer, and reality television personality who has a net worth of $5 million.
Long before becoming a reality television star, Carole Radziwill spent nearly two decades in broadcast journalism, primarily at ABC News, where she worked with Peter Jennings and covered stories ranging from American politics and social issues to conflicts overseas. Her journalism earned her three Emmy Awards, a Peabody Award, a Robert F. Kennedy Humanitarian Award, and a GLAAD Media Award.
Radziwill later became a bestselling author with her 2005 memoir, ""What Remains: A Memoir of Fate, Friendship and Love,"" which chronicled the death of her husband, Anthony Radziwill, as well as her close friendship with John F. Kennedy Jr. and Carolyn Bessette-Kennedy. She reached a much larger television audience after joining Bravo's ""The Real Housewives of New York City"" in 2012, remaining on the series for six seasons before leaving in 2018. In the years that followed, she returned her attention to writing, production, and other media projects while remaining closely associated with the ""Real Housewives"" franchise.
Carole Ann DiFalco was born on August 20, 1963. She grew up in Suffern, New York, in a working-class family and later moved to New York City for college. She earned a bachelor's degree from Hunter College and later completed an MBA at New York University.
Radziwill did not grow up in the wealthy or socially prominent circles with which she would eventually become associated. Her entry into that world came through her television career and later her marriage to Anthony Radziwill, but she had already begun establishing herself professionally before the two met. Her early years in journalism ultimately became the foundation of a career that would span television news, publishing, reality television, and production.
Radziwill began her career at ABC News as an intern in postproduction for the newsmagazine ""20/20."" She subsequently worked as a production secretary on ""Close Up"" before joining Peter Jennings' documentary unit. There, she produced and reported stories dealing with subjects including abortion, gun control, medical issues, and international affairs.
Her assignments eventually took her around the world. Radziwill reported from countries including Cambodia, Haiti, and India and spent time in Israel during the Gulf War, covering the aftermath of Iraqi Scud missile attacks. More than a decade later, she returned to overseas conflict reporting and spent several weeks in Afghanistan embedded with members of the 101st Airborne Division while working on ABC's ""Profiles from the Front Line.""
Radziwill's journalism earned her multiple professional honors, including three Emmy Awards. One of her award-winning projects focused on Vietnam veteran Bobby Muller's campaign against landmines in Cambodia, while other reporting brought recognition from organizations including the Peabody Awards and GLAAD. By the time she left full-time network news, she had built a substantial career that was largely unrelated to the reality television fame she would later achieve.
While working at ABC News, Carole met fellow television producer Anthony Radziwill . They married on August 27, 1994, in East Hampton, New York. Anthony belonged to the prominent Polish Radziwiłł family and also had close ties to one of America's most famous political families.
Anthony's mother, Lee Radziwill , was the younger sister of former First Lady Jacqueline Kennedy Onassis . That made Anthony a first cousin of Caroline Kennedy and John F. Kennedy Jr. Carole consequently became part of a close social and family circle that included John and his wife, Carolyn Bessette-Kennedy.
Anthony had initially been diagnosed with testicular cancer in 1989, several years before he and Carole married. His cancer later returned and metastasized, and Carole became his primary caregiver during much of their marriage. He died on August 10, 1999, at the age of 40 after years of treatment.
His death came only a few weeks after another devastating loss. John F. Kennedy Jr., Carolyn Bessette-Kennedy, and Carolyn's sister Lauren Bessette were killed in a plane crash on July 16, 1999. Carole had been especially close to Carolyn, whom she described as her best friend, while Anthony and John were both cousins and close friends.
After Anthony's death, Radziwill eventually left ABC News and began writing about the extraordinary period of her life surrounding her marriage, her husband's illness, and the deaths of those closest to her. The result was ""What Remains: A Memoir of Fate, Friendship and Love,"" published in 2005. The book combined her upbringing and journalism career with an intimate account of Anthony's cancer battle and the intertwined relationships between the Radziwill and Kennedy families.
""What Remains"" became a New York Times bestseller and remained on the list for an extended period. The memoir established Radziwill as a successful writer independent of both her journalism career and the famous family into which she had married. It remains one of the most significant professional achievements of her career and later experienced renewed interest as public fascination with John F. Kennedy Jr. and Carolyn Bessette-Kennedy continued.
Following the success of her memoir, Radziwill became a contributing writer for ""Glamour"" magazine. She wrote a recurring feature called ""Lunch Date,"" in which she interviewed celebrities and other notable figures over meals. Her subjects included Antonio Banderas , Alec Baldwin , Rudy Giuliani , and a range of people from entertainment, media, and politics.
In 2014, Radziwill published her first novel, ""The Widow's Guide to Sex & Dating."" The dark comedy centers on a woman rebuilding her life after the sudden death of her husband and naturally drew comparisons to aspects of Radziwill's own experience. She reportedly received a six-figure publishing deal for the book, and its screen rights were later optioned for television development.
Radziwill has continued writing personal essays and commentary beyond her books. She has participated in live storytelling events, including appearances with The Moth, and launched the newsletter ""The Voice of Reason with Carole Radziwill."" Her writing frequently draws on her experiences with widowhood, New York society, television, travel, relationships, and the media.
Radziwill joined the cast of ""The Real Housewives of New York City"" during the show's fifth season in 2012. Her background as an Emmy-winning journalist and bestselling author distinguished her from many of the franchise's other personalities, and she often approached the show's conflicts with a dry, skeptical sense of humor. Her storylines covered friendships, travel, dating, writing projects, and the unusual circumstances of her earlier life.
She remained with ""RHONY"" for six seasons, appearing from Season 5 through Season 10. One of her most prominent later storylines involved the deterioration of her once-close friendship with Bethenny Frankel , which became a major focus of her final season. Radziwill announced her departure in 2018, saying she wanted to return her attention to journalism, writing, and producing.
Her years on ""The Real Housewives of New York City"" nevertheless became a defining second act in her career. The show introduced her to an audience far larger than the one familiar with her ABC News work or books, and she has continued to discuss the franchise and her experience with reality television in interviews and writing. Her tenure is frequently remembered for the contrast between her understated personality and the more theatrical style of the series.
Radziwill has continued working behind the scenes in entertainment in addition to writing and appearing on television. She formed Radzi Media and pursued television, documentary, and scripted projects, including adaptations connected with her own books and experiences. Her production ambitions reflected a return to the behind-the-camera work that had defined the earliest part of her career.
She has also remained involved in New York's cultural world through theater, storytelling, and media projects. Rather than relying exclusively on reality television, Radziwill has moved between journalism, publishing, television production, and live storytelling throughout her career. That broader résumé helps distinguish her from many personalities who became famous primarily through the ""Real Housewives"" franchise.
Several years after Anthony's death, Radziwill dated chef Adam Kenworthy. Their relationship became a recurring storyline on ""The Real Housewives of New York City,"" in part because Kenworthy is more than 20 years younger than Radziwill. They dated on and off from approximately 2014 until 2017 and remained friendly after their romantic relationship ended.
Radziwill has not remarried. She has continued to speak and write openly about widowhood, dating, grief, and the complications of rebuilding a life after losing a spouse at a relatively young age. Her marriage to Anthony and her friendships with John F. Kennedy Jr. and Carolyn Bessette-Kennedy have also remained recurring subjects of public interest.
After the arrest of Ghislaine Maxwell , older photographs showing Radziwill with Maxwell circulated publicly. Radziwill's contact information also appeared in an address book associated with convicted sex offender Jeffrey Epstein . Radziwill said that she had been socially friendly with Maxwell in the early 2000s but had not communicated with her for many years, and she stated that she did not know Epstein personally or have knowledge of his criminal activities.
Radziwill has lived for more than two decades in a Manhattan apartment in the SoHo neighborhood. She purchased the property for approximately $975,000 in 2002, several years after Anthony's death. The home became familiar to viewers because it appeared repeatedly during her six seasons on ""The Real Housewives of New York City.""
Radziwill has renovated and redesigned the apartment several times, updating its floors, kitchen, bedroom, and living spaces while maintaining some distinctive pieces with sentimental value. One of the best-known items is a vintage tiger-striped sofa that previously belonged to her mother-in-law, Lee Radziwill. Before purchasing the SoHo property, Carole and Anthony lived together in an apartment on Park Avenue.",ok
gossipcop-931325,https://www.etonline.com/johnny-depp-sued-by-ex-bodyguards-for-unsafe-working-conditions-and-unpaid-wages-101470,Johnny Depp Sued by Ex-Bodyguards for Unsafe Working Conditions and Unpaid Wages,991439353376264192	991441147330428930	991441383578832896	991444507613855745	991450935334322176	991456473623359488	991498416424472576	991506140717633538	991506170593595392	991506324625281024	991506344954998789	991506359479828482	991506373295988738	991506394380750848	991506425498210304	991506580221906944	991506604313935878	991506629794418689	991506638141120514	991506719560929282	991506847034114048	991506858065186816	991506923181805568	991506996980568064	991507088718393344	991507094921719808	991507103494955008	991507118934151168	991507171878866945	991507210399371264	991507336085819397	991507353286701056	991507364342886400	991507475340910592	991573532692877312	991575039702175744	991589356703010816	991606069272211456	991761858188234753	992435210489946114	992439726174072837	992457076994707457,0,etonline.com,80,"Johnny Depp is facing a new lawsuit filed by two former bodyguards, who are suing the Pirates of the Caribbean star for unpaid wages, wrongful termination and unlawful business practices, among numerous other allegations.
In documents obtained by ET, private security professionals Eugene Arreola and Miguel Sanchez say the were employees of the private security firm  Premiere Group International and claim they were assigned to protect Depp.
When the 54-year-old actor faced a ""financial crisis"" in 2016 , Depp cut ties with the company, but hired both Arreola and Sanchez individually, according to the lawsuit. They claim that they were ""told they would be classified as ""employees,"" and would receive all the protections and benefits they were entitled to under the law.""
The bodyguards -- who claim that they formed close friendships with Depp's family and friends during their years guarding the movie star -- allege that, between 2016 and 2018, ""things began to spiral toward chaos,"" and that they ""found themselves in situations that required more than what a bodyguard would be expected to do.""
The lawsuit alleges that the bodyguards were ""asked repeatedly to drive vehicles that contained illegal substances, open containers and minors"" and were ""asked to monitor unstable individuals in [Depp's] life and entourage.""
The suit further alleges that Arreola and Sanchez were ""forced to protect [Depp] from himself and his vices while in public, becoming caretakers for him.""
Additionally, the bodyguards claim that they were ""exposed to unsafe conditions and situations"" and, as a result, they suffered ""stress-related injuries created by a hostile and unsafe work environment.""
Arreola and Sanchez also claim that, for nearly two years, neither of them were paid overtime wages, despite working 12-hour shifts. They also claim that they were not given their legally mandated meal and rest breaks.
Many of the claims in the suit -- particularly allegations of the ""toxic and dangerous work environment and constant labor code violations"" -- are directed at the head of Depp's private security detail, Leonard Damian, who is claimed in the documents to have been their direct supervisor.
The documents also detail an incident in which Damian allegedly accidentally shot himself in the leg ""while playing with his weapon in a small control room"" on Depp's property, when Sanchez says he ""had no choice"" but to rush Damian to the hospital.
The documents allege that ""[Arreola and Sanchez] were forced to leave their jobs despite claiming that they enjoyed many of the people they worked with and had no ill will toward Johnny Depp.""
